WEBVTT

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:01.640
<v Ira Glass>Zoe Chace.
<v Ira Glass>佐伊·蔡斯。

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:02.510
<v Zoe Chace>Ira Glass.
<v Zoe Chace>艾拉·格拉斯。

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:03.530
<v Ira Glass>So you went to Iowa.
<v Ira Glass>所以你去爱荷华州了。

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:09.630
<v Zoe Chace>I did. You know, there are like 50,000 Democratic candidates running for president. They're all in Iowa.
<v Zoe Chace>是的。你知道，有大约50,000名民主党候选人正在竞选总统。他们都在爱荷华州。

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.330
<v Ira Glass>It's what? It's 25 right now.
<v Ira Glass>是多少？现在有25个了。

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:12.320
<v Zoe Chace>It's 25 right now.
<v Zoe Chace>现在有25个。

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:12.920
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:29.400
<v Zoe Chace>And there's this one political writer, Dave Weigel with the Washington Post, he writes this newsletter thing called The Trailer three times a week. And he has this particular superpower where somehow, he seems to be with every single candidate who's running at the exact same time.
<v Zoe Chace>还有一位政治作家，《华盛顿邮报》的戴夫·维格尔，他每周写三次名为《预告片》的通讯。他有一种特殊的天赋，不知何故，他似乎总能同时出现在每一位正在参选的候选人身边。

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:47.820
<v Zoe Chace>So at this moment, where everything seems like this grand 25 ring circus with this race, he seemed like maybe the very best person in the world to watch this with. When there's the next president, he'll have seen every moment, not skipping anything. Hopefully, he will understand why it happened.
<v Zoe Chace>所以在这一刻，当这场竞选看起来像是一场宏大的25环马戏团表演时，他似乎是世界上最适合一起观看这一切的人。当下一任总统产生时，他将见证每一个时刻，不遗漏任何东西。希望他能理解这一切发生的原因。

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.590
<v Ira Glass>And so you basically wanted to see the whole election all at once the way he does.
<v Ira Glass>所以你基本上是想和他一样，一次性看完整个选举过程。

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:59.960
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah. And it's so crazy traveling through primary states with him. He knows these places so well. In Iowa, we pulled up to a gas station.
<v Zoe Chace>是的。和他一起走访初选州真是太疯狂了。他非常了解这些地方。在爱荷华州，我们把车开到了一家加油站。

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:09.500
<v Dave Weigel>If I had to rank Iowa gas stations, I think QuikTrip, then Kum & Go, then Casey's.
<v Dave Weigel>如果非要给爱荷华州的加油站排名，我想是QuikTrip，然后是Kum & Go，最后是Casey's。

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:15.550
<v Zoe Chace>Wait. So what's so great about QuikTrip?
<v Zoe Chace>等等。QuikTrip有什么好的？

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:30.520
<v Dave Weigel>The current ones, the fairly new ones-- there are some that have been there for a while. But the new ones, I think it might have high quality lids that are like high quality plastic, as opposed to the thing that just breaks--
<v Dave Weigel>现在的那些，比较新的那些——有些已经开了很久了。但我认为新的那些，可能杯盖的质量很高，是那种高质量的塑料，而不是那种用一次就坏的——

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:30.840
<v Zoe Chace>Lids?
<v Zoe Chace>杯盖？

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:41.640
<v Dave Weigel>--after one use. Yeah, lids. And keep in mind. This is the only taste I'm going to have for a few hours, and I need the caffeine. So I've put thought into it. And I have colleagues who are--
<v Dave Weigel>——用一次就坏。是的，杯盖。记住，这可能是我几个小时内唯一能喝到的东西，我需要咖啡因。所以我考虑过这个问题。我有一些同事——

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.810
<v Zoe Chace>We were leaving a Hickenlooper event and racing over to a Beto event.
<v Zoe Chace>我们要离开一场希肯卢珀的活动，赶往一场贝托的活动。

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:58.920
<v Ira Glass>Beto is a former Texas senatorial candidate, Beto O'Rourke. Hickenlooper, John Hickenlooper, former Colorado governor-- it took me so long to read up on all these guys. It took like a day to figure out who's who. Yes.
<v Ira Glass>贝托是前得克萨斯州参议员候选人，贝托·奥罗克。希肯卢珀，约翰·希肯卢珀，前科罗拉多州州长——我花了好长时间才读完关于这些人的资料。我花了一整天时间才弄清楚谁是谁。是的。

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:20.630
<v Zoe Chace>Thank you for that. We flew in. We went to four things in a day. First, Knoxville, Iowa. Weigel looks kind of like a reporter from the '70s-- mustached, notably, casually dressed, hair askew. We're going to this tiny event at this tiny brewpub. And as we're driving up to it, we think we see the candidate standing right outside.
<v Zoe Chace>谢谢你。我们飞了过来。我们一天参加了四场活动。首先是爱荷华州的诺克斯维尔。维格尔看起来有点像70年代的记者——留着胡子，穿着随便，头发蓬乱。我们要去一家小酒吧参加一场小型活动。当我们开车过去时，我们以为看到候选人正站在外面。

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.740
<v Dave Weigel>It does look like Beto. You're right.
<v Dave Weigel>那看起来确实是贝托。你是对的。

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.290
<v Zoe Chace>That guy?
<v Zoe Chace>那个人？

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:23.790
<v Dave Weigel>Yeah.
<v Dave Weigel>是的。

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.240
<v Zoe Chace>I think it's him.
<v Zoe Chace>我觉得是他。

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:44.690
<v Dave Weigel>He's fairly tall. Huh. That's the outfit, the chinos and everything. That is him. Isn't it surreal? Just like when you're walking through a small town, you're like, oh, that guy running for president. Yeah, all right.
<v Dave Weigel>他挺高的。呵。那身行头，卡其裤什么的。那是他。这不是很超现实吗？就像当你走在小镇上，你会想，哦，那个正在竞选总统的人。是的，好吧。

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:50.250
<v Zoe Chace>I don't know why it's surreal to him. This happens to him literally all the time.
<v Zoe Chace>我不知道为什么对他来说这很超现实。这种事对他来说简直是家常便饭。

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:57.120
<v Dave Weigel>I think he's waiting to be introduced. Oh, Amy. That's his wife, who's with him today-- the one woman in the--
<v Dave Weigel>我想他是在等被介绍。哦，艾米。那是他今天和他一起的妻子——那个女人在——

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:57.740
<v Zoe Chace>In that group?
<v Zoe Chace>在那群人里？

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:04.330
<v Dave Weigel>Yeah. [CHUCKLES]
<v Dave Weigel>是的。[轻笑]

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.100
<v Zoe Chace>What?
<v Zoe Chace>什么？

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.510
<v Dave Weigel>I don't know. Just, it's fun living through things like this.
<v Dave Weigel>不知道。只是，经历这样的事情很有趣。

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.650
<v Ira Glass>Oh, he's really into it.
<v Ira Glass>哦，他真的很投入。

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:40.570
<v Zoe Chace>It thrills him. He loves this. There's this one moment after Beto finishes his little talk, and people come up to him and ask questions. And this guy-- it's in this tiny brewery, which kind of looks like a rec room, almost. And this one voter walks up to Beto with a beer in his hand, just like as though he's at some kind of relaxed Friday afternoon cocktail party. And Weigel sees this, and jumps up and kind of elbows people out of the way.
<v Zoe Chace>这让他感到兴奋。他喜欢这样。有一次，在贝托结束演讲后，人们上来问他问题。那个家伙——就在这个小酿酒厂里，看起来几乎像个休息室。这个选民拿着啤酒走到贝托面前，就像在周五下午的鸡尾酒会上一样轻松。维格尔看到了这一点，跳起来，把人们挤到一边。

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:44.660
<v Dave Weigel>Sorry, I'm just trying to get a picture of the guy holding the beer while talking to him, just because it amuses me.
<v Dave Weigel>抱歉，我只是想给那个拿着啤酒和他在说话的人拍张照，因为这让我觉得很有趣。

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:45.760
<v Man>Oh. Yeah, sure.
<v Man>哦。好的，没问题。

00:03:45.760 --> 00:04:10.110
<v Zoe Chace>It kind of exemplifies the special part we're in, in the election. There's no Secret Service. There are no real barriers. Anyone can just wander up to a presidential candidate. It's extremely intimate. It's his favorite time of the presidential elections. People are really close to each other. At one point at another event, two of the candidates, Tim Ryan and Jay Inslee, they ran right past each other.
<v Zoe Chace>这在某种程度上体现了我们目前所处的选举阶段。没有特勤局，没有真正的障碍。任何人都可以随意走到总统候选人面前。这非常亲密。这是他最喜欢的总统选举时刻。人们彼此之间非常亲近。有一次在另一场活动中，两位候选人，蒂姆·瑞安和杰伊·英斯利，他们刚好跑过彼此。

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.510
<v Man 1>You're back in the district.
<v Man 1>你回到选区了。

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:12.540
<v Man 2>Go get them, Congressman Ryan!
<v Man 2>加油，瑞安众议员！

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.010
<v Man 3>You did a great job, Governor! I love your speech!
<v Man 3>你做得太棒了，州长！我喜欢你的演讲！

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:16.330
<v Man 2>You're awesome!
<v Man 2>你太棒了！

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:19.800
<v Man 3>Do it again! I want to hear it one more time.
<v Man 3>再来一次！我想再听一遍。

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:32.760
<v Zoe Chace>It's almost like Weigel and the candidates are on some kind of a cruise or at a camp together. And they just bump into each other day after day, like, oh, hi again. Oh, I'll see you tomorrow. Oh, I'll see you later. Are you going to be at the pizza thing?
<v Zoe Chace>感觉就像维格尔和这些候选人都在游轮上或者是营地里。他们日复一日地相遇，就像，哦，又见面了。哦，明天见。哦，稍后见。你要去吃披萨吗？

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:33.160
<v Man 1>Thank you.
<v Man 1>谢谢。

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:34.420
<v Man 2>Thanks a lot. Good to see you again.
<v Man 2>非常感谢。很高兴再次见到你。

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:34.890
<v Man 1>Yeah. Good to see you.
<v Man 1>是的。很高兴见到你。

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.260
<v Man 2>See you at the-- I will not catch up with you in the run. But I'll--
<v Man 2>回头见——我不会在竞选路上追上你的。但我会——

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:38.070
<v Man 1>Are you going to run it?
<v Man 1>你要参选吗？

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:44.550
<v Zoe Chace>You also, in this phase of things, you get to see the candidates interacting in the world, the regular world.
<v Zoe Chace>在这个阶段，你还可以看到候选人在现实世界中互动。

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:44.880
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>没错。

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.870
<v Zoe Chace>Regular people with just the most random stuff that you wouldn't normally see.
<v Zoe Chace>普通人会遇到你平时看不到的随机事情。

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:49.560
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:54.750
<v Zoe Chace>I had this thing happened to me. I was checking into our hotel in Waterloo, Iowa, right?
<v Zoe Chace>我遇到过这件事。我在爱荷华州滑铁卢的酒店办理入住，对吧？

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:55.800
<v Ira Glass>What kind of hotel?
<v Ira Glass>什么样的酒店？

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:56.500
<v Zoe Chace>Hampton Inn.
<v Zoe Chace>汉普顿酒店。

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:56.970
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:16.650
<v Zoe Chace>And the girl who's checking us in, who's like 20-ish maybe-- she seems funny and smiley. And she's really surprised to see these reporters there. And it turns out she's never heard of the Iowa caucuses. Even though the state is crawling with these candidates, it's totally possible to lead your life and miss it. So Weigel explains it to her.
<v Zoe Chace>负责办理入住的女孩，看起来大概20岁出头——她看起来很有趣，脸上带着笑容。看到这些记者在那里，她感到非常惊讶。结果她从来没听说过爱荷华州党团会议。尽管这个州到处都是这些候选人，但完全有可能过着自己的生活却错过了这一切。所以维格尔向她解释了。

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.840
<v Dave Weigel>Yeah, instead of a presidential primary here--
<v Dave Weigel>是的，代替这里的总统初选——

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:42.890
<v Zoe Chace>All these presidential candidates for the nation to vote, blah, blah, blah. We go upstairs. But my key is demagnetized, so I go back downstairs. I leave all my stuff, including the recorder, there. And when I get downstairs, I see Beto just standing there. And he's trying to get a free bottle of water from the girl. And I'm like, Beto?
<v Zoe Chace>所有这些国家总统候选人都在投票，等等。我们上楼了。但我的房卡消磁了，所以我又回到了楼下。我把所有东西，包括录音机，都留在了那里。当我下楼时，我看到贝托正站在那儿。他正试图从那个女孩那里免费要一瓶水。我当时就想，贝托？

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:44.040
<v Ira Glass>Wait, do you know him?
<v Ira Glass>等等，你认识他吗？

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:44.520
<v Zoe Chace>No.
<v Zoe Chace>不认识。

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:45.120
<v Ira Glass>You've never met him.
<v Ira Glass>你从来没见过他。

00:05:45.120 --> 00:06:29.890
<v Zoe Chace>No. And I say to him, Beto, tell her what you're doing here in Iowa. And she looks at him, and she goes, are you running for president, in this totally sarcastic way. Like of course you're not. And he goes, actually, yes, I am, which is just a weird answer to that question. And she jumps up, goes, yes, queen! And he's like totally taken aback and is like, thank you so much. And she goes, I hope you win the presidency-- like not very sincere.
<v Zoe Chace>没有。我对他说，贝托，告诉她你在这儿爱荷华州干什么。她看着他，讽刺地说，你在竞选总统吗？就好像他当然不是。他说，实际上，是的，我确实是，这对他那个问题的回答太奇怪了。她跳起来说，是的，女王！他完全被吓到了，说，非常感谢。她说，我希望你赢得总统大选——听起来不太真诚。

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:32.620
<v Ira Glass>And so this is just the world that Weigel was marinating in.
<v Ira Glass>这就是维格尔所处的环境。

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:46.840
<v Zoe Chace>He knows them all so thoroughly at this point. He knows them as thoroughly as he knows the gas stations in Iowa. We were at a bar. He starts talking about the jokes that candidates tell when they're out on [INAUDIBLE].
<v Zoe Chace>此时此刻，他非常了解他们所有人。他了解他们就像了解爱荷华州的加油站一样彻底。我们在一家酒吧。他开始谈论候选人在外面时讲的笑话。

00:06:46.840 --> 00:07:14.830
<v Dave Weigel>The horrible spouse joke. Whereas, Warren has a riff where she talks about dropping out of school. It's like, fell in love. Woo-hoo. Got married, Woo-hoo. Had a baby. Woo. And she just-- it's like a Simpsons joke, almost. She imitates getting less and less excited about her bad life decisions. She's like, and then I met my first husband. Never a good idea when you have to number them, things like that.
<v Dave Weigel>糟糕的配偶笑话。而沃伦有一个即兴表演，她谈论辍学。就像，坠入爱河。呜呼。结婚了，呜呼。生了个孩子。呜。然后她只是——就像辛普森一家的笑话一样。她模仿自己对糟糕的人生决定越来越不兴奋的样子。她说，然后我遇到了我的第一任丈夫。当你必须给他们编号时，这从来都不是个好主意，诸如此类。

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:41.360
<v Dave Weigel>My favorite Klobuchar joke is, she talks about how she's Slovenian American. And it's like, and for years, I was the most famous Slovenian American in politics. But then Melania Trump arrived, and you know what? It's like looking in a mirror. You laugh. It's like a [INAUDIBLE]. But Biden does the, my wife is always right joke. Who else does that? People mostly learn to not do that.
<v Dave Weigel>我最喜欢的克洛布查笑话是，她谈论自己是斯洛文尼亚裔美国人。她说，多年来，我是政界最著名的斯洛文尼亚裔美国人。但后来梅拉尼娅·特朗普来了，你知道吗？这就像照镜子。你笑了起来。这就像一个[听不清]。但拜登讲那个“我妻子永远是对的”的笑话。还有谁讲那个？人们大多学会了不那样做。

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:52.810
<v Ira Glass>For today on our program, we're in this unusual political moment. The Democrats have even more people running for president than the Republicans did last time. 25 people-- what is that?
<v Ira Glass>在今天的节目中，我们正处于一个不同寻常的政治时刻。民主党参选总统的人数甚至比共和党上次还要多。25个人——那是什么情况？

00:07:52.810 --> 00:08:06.470
<v Ira Glass>Today in our show, we are not going to cover all 25. What we're going to do is we're going to drop in here and there. My sense is that, at this point, everyone is still getting to know who these people are. And so hopefully, you will get in close with some of them and get an impression of them.
<v Ira Glass>在今天的节目中，我们不会涵盖所有25个人。我们要做的就是四处走走。我觉得，在这一点上，每个人还在了解这些人是谁。所以希望你能近距离观察他们中的一些人，并对他们有所了解。

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:50.150
<v Ira Glass>And what we're especially interested in in this show is all of the candidates at the bottom. That's the story of this particular moment in the election, is all those people between 0% and 1% in the polls who might still have a chance trying to get noticed and break out of the pack, and join the front runners, which is just an enormously difficult thing to do. And it's just right now starting to shift. How do you get noticed and rise above 1% in this pack of 25? From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>在这个节目中，我们特别感兴趣的是底层的所有候选人。这是这次选举特殊时刻的故事，是那些在民调中处于0%到1%之间的人，他们可能还有机会努力被注意到，从人群中脱颖而出，加入领跑者的行列，这是一件极其困难的事情。目前局势才刚刚开始转变。在这25人的队伍中，你如何引起注意并升至1%以上？来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:50.470
<v Ira Glass>Act 1.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:53.460
<v Zoe Chace>No. No acts today. Let's just drive around.
<v Zoe Chace>不。今天没有表演。我们开车兜兜风吧。

00:08:53.460 --> 00:09:13.590
<v Ira Glass>So bossy. All right, ma'am. So Zoe, so 25 candidates-- and this political operative explained this to me, and I found it a helpful way to look at this. He said that the best way to picture what is happening right now is that there are four front runners-- Joe Biden, Bernie Sanders, Elizabeth Warren, and Kamala Harris. And then depending on how you count it, Pete Buttigieg is in there, too.
<v Ira Glass>真霸道。好吧，女士。那么佐伊，25位候选人——这位政治运作人员向我解释过，我觉得这是一种很有帮助的看待方式。他说，描述现在发生的事情最好的方式是，有四位领跑者——乔·拜登、伯尼·桑德斯、伊丽莎白·沃伦和卡玛拉·哈里斯。然后根据你的计算方式，皮特·布蒂吉格也在其中。

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:28.140
<v Ira Glass>Their poll numbers are way higher than anybody else. And also, they get most of the donations and most of the press attention, which then generates more donations and more press attention in a kind of self-perpetuating cycle that sucks the air out that all the others want to breathe.
<v Ira Glass>他们的民调数字远高于其他人。此外，他们还获得了大部分捐款和媒体关注，这又产生更多的捐款和媒体关注，形成一种自我强化的循环，吸干了其他人想要呼吸的空气。

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:43.020
<v Ira Glass>So that's them. And then below those four or five people, there's basically everyone else in the pack of 25, most of them struggling to break 1%. And it seems just very interesting what is happening with them. And so, Zoe, you watched them trying to figure that out.
<v Ira Glass>所以那是他们。然后在这四五个人之下，基本上就是25人队伍中的其他人，他们中的大多数都在努力突破1%。而且目前发生在他们身上的事情看起来非常有趣。所以，佐伊，你看着他们试图弄清楚这一点。

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:46.830
<v Zoe Chace>OK. So for example, take John Hickenlooper.
<v Zoe Chace>好的。比如，以约翰·希肯卢珀为例。

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:48.970
<v Ira Glass>Former governor of Colorado.
<v Ira Glass>科罗拉多州前州长。

00:09:48.970 --> 00:10:02.810
<v Zoe Chace>His polling is now at less than 1%. If you haven't heard of him, that's probably why. He cannot draw a crowd by himself. If he put out a sign in Iowa today, Hickenlooper Speaks, Please RSVP, probably nobody would come to that event.
<v Zoe Chace>他的民调现在不到1%。如果你没听说过他，这就是原因。他自己一个人无法吸引人群。如果他今天在爱荷华州挂出一块牌子，上面写着“希肯卢珀演讲，请回复”，可能根本没人会来参加那场活动。

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:04.080
<v Ira Glass>Crickets, right? Yeah. OK.
<v Ira Glass>鸦雀无声，对吧？是的。好的。

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:16.230
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah. So he does these-- they're called retail stops, when a politician tours a factory or whatever. So right now, we're at this brand new distillery in Des Moines, me and Dave Weigel.
<v Zoe Chace>是的。所以他做这些——他们被称为零售站点，也就是政治家参观工厂之类的地方。所以现在，我们正在得梅因一家全新的酿酒厂，我和戴夫·维格尔在一起。

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:19.610
<v Dave Weigel>--because this stuff is actually kind of fun to watch.
<v Dave Weigel>——因为看这些东西实际上挺有趣的。

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:38.070
<v Zoe Chace>And we walk in. Hickenlooper and this one elderly Iowa voter are sitting at the bar, the brand new bar in the brand new distillery. It's a totally staged photo op. There are about 20 reporters in a semicircle around them. But obviously, if you look through the camera lens, it looks like it's just Hickenlooper and the voter.
<v Zoe Chace>我们走了进去。希肯卢珀和一位年迈的爱荷华州选民正坐在酒吧里，那是全新酿酒厂里的全新酒吧。这完全是摆拍的摄影机会。周围大约有20名记者围成一个半圆。但很显然，如果你通过镜头看，看起来就像只有希肯卢珀和那位选民。

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:42.140
<v John Hickenlooper>What a pleasure. I can't tell you. This is an absolute honor.
<v John Hickenlooper>真是荣幸。我无法形容。这绝对是一种荣誉。

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:43.380
<v Man>You're running for what?
<v Man>你在竞选什么？

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:45.510
<v Zoe Chace>The old guy's like, you're running for what?
<v Zoe Chace>那位老人说，你在竞选什么？

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.320
<v John Hickenlooper>The highest office of the land-- President of the United States.
<v John Hickenlooper>国家的最高职位——美国总统。

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:49.140
<v Man>Oh, really? OK.
<v Man>哦，真的吗？好吧。

00:10:49.140 --> 00:11:03.840
<v John Hickenlooper>So I was an entrepreneur. I built brew pubs all over the-- mostly over the Midwest. But I built the biggest brew pub in the country, and Denver was my first one. Always in abandoned warehouses. I won the Award of Honor from the National Trust for Historic Preservation. I don't know what that's good for.
<v John Hickenlooper>我曾是一名企业家。我在整个——主要是在中西部——建造了酿酒厂。但我建造了全国最大的酿酒厂，丹佛是我的第一个。总是在废弃的仓库里。我获得了国家历史保护信托基金的荣誉奖。我不知道那有什么用。

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:09.590
<v Ira Glass>It's so strange that these guys actually-- they do have to kind of just brag about themselves to people, huh?
<v Ira Glass>这些家伙实际上不得不向人们吹嘘自己，真是太奇怪了，对吧？

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:09.830
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah.
<v Zoe Chace>是的。

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:17.310
<v Ira Glass>Like people don't know who they are. I mean, not to be mean about it, but people [INAUDIBLE] don't know who they are. So they just have to say, here's all the stuff I've done.
<v Ira Glass>就像人们不知道他们是谁一样。我的意思是，不是要针对他们，但人们[听不清]不知道他们是谁。所以他们只能说，这是我做过的所有事情。

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:19.590
<v Zoe Chace>Even he gets kind of self-conscious about it.
<v Zoe Chace>连他自己都对此感到有点尴尬。

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:25.770
<v John Hickenlooper>I call it the fundamental nonsense of Washington, or place of common sense. That's my shtick. But anyway--
<v John Hickenlooper>我称之为华盛顿的基本废话，或者常识之地。那是我的拿手好戏。但无论如何——

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:26.700
<v Ira Glass>That's my shtick?
<v Ira Glass>那是我的拿手好戏？

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:27.830
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah.
<v Zoe Chace>是的。

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:29.130
<v Ira Glass>Oh, I like that.
<v Ira Glass>哦，我喜欢这个。

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:30.000
<v Man>Good luck.
<v Man>祝你好运。

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:45.570
<v Zoe Chace>Good luck, the old guy says to him. 20 people are recording this, and no one is going to use any of this. No one's going to use these quotes, right? We move on to a distillery tour. Hickenlooper has tons of opinions about that. Remember, he was in this business.
<v Zoe Chace>祝你好运，老人对他说。20个人正在录制这一幕，没人会用这段内容。没人会用这些引语，对吧？我们继续进行酿酒厂参观。希肯卢珀对此有很多意见。记住，他以前是干这一行的。

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:48.270
<v John Hickenlooper>I always call it the Haagen-Dazs effect, which is--
<v John Hickenlooper>我总是称之为哈根达斯效应，也就是——

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:57.550
<v Zoe Chace>No one is going to use any of this either. Instead, the reporters just wait til the end. They gather around, they ask him questions about Trump. That stuff, they might use.
<v Zoe Chace>也没人会用这段。相反，记者们只是等到最后。他们围过来，问他关于特朗普的问题。那些东西，他们可能会用。

00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:00.270
<v Ira Glass>So this whole thing, this whole tour, all of it is just for pictures.
<v Ira Glass>所以整件事，整个行程，全部都是为了拍照。

00:12:00.270 --> 00:12:09.000
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah. What the campaign hopes for is that, out of the 25 candidates, tonight viewers will see a picture somewhere of John Hickenlooper drinking a beer in Iowa.
<v Zoe Chace>是的。竞选团队希望的是，在25位候选人中，今晚观众会在某处看到一张约翰·希肯卢珀在爱荷华州喝啤酒的照片。

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:17.430
<v Ira Glass>I don't see how that's going to make him president. That is not going to be enough to take him out of the 1% and put him up there with the front runners.
<v Ira Glass>我不明白这怎么能让他成为总统。那不足以让他摆脱1%，并让他与领跑者并驾齐驱。

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:44.580
<v Zoe Chace>Iowa, though-- voter by voter. These 1%-ers, they're also going around Iowa talking, and talking, and talking to the actual voters, the real human beings who go to the caucuses and vote. And in order to do that, the candidates who can't draw a crowd on their own, one thing those guys do is go to local Democratic clubs. The idea is you do these Democratic clubs for a while, county Democratic clubs, and then you get popular enough to graduate out and draw a crowd on your own.
<v Zoe Chace>不过，爱荷华州——选民逐个击破。这些1%的人，他们也正在爱荷华州到处走动，与实际选民、参加党团会议并投票的真正人类交谈、交谈再交谈。为了做到这一点，那些无法独立吸引人群的候选人，他们做的一件事就是去当地的民主党俱乐部。他们的想法是，你先去这些民主党俱乐部一段时间，比如县民主党俱乐部，然后你就会变得足够受欢迎，从而脱颖而出并独立吸引人群。

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:45.420
<v Ira Glass>Ah.
<v Ira Glass>啊。

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:56.060
<v Zoe Chace>Like Pete Buttigieg, he had to do the Democratic clubs for about a month. He went around introducing himself, and then he graduated out. Now he can draw a crowd on his own. Most of them have not.
<v Zoe Chace>就像皮特·布蒂吉格，他不得不参加了大约一个月的民主党俱乐部活动。他四处介绍自己，然后他脱颖而出。现在他可以独立吸引人群了。他们中的大多数还没有做到。

00:12:56.060 --> 00:13:00.150
<v Dave Weigel>I remember-- there's a Delaney bumper sticker. They're real.
<v Dave Weigel>我记得——有一个德莱尼的保险杠贴纸。它们是真的。

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:00.630
<v Zoe Chace>Whoa.
<v Zoe Chace>哇。

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:02.130
<v Dave Weigel>There was one that was--
<v Dave Weigel>有一个是——

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:03.770
<v Zoe Chace>What if that's Delaney's car, though?
<v Zoe Chace>如果那是德莱尼的车呢？

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:04.590
<v Dave Weigel>It could be.
<v Dave Weigel>有可能。

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:15.870
<v Zoe Chace>John Delaney, also at less than 1%, rich guy and former congressman from Maryland. He's doing a meet-and-greet with the Clive County Democrats at Wobbly Boots Barbecue.
<v Zoe Chace>约翰·德莱尼，支持率也低于1%，是有钱人，来自马里兰州的前众议员。他正在沃布里靴子烧烤店与克莱夫县民主党人进行见面会。

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:20.810
<v John Delaney>So I'm sure this is going to surprise you. I actually believe I have the best [INAUDIBLE].
<v John Delaney>所以我敢肯定这会让你感到惊讶。我实际上相信我有最好的[听不清]。

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:23.030
<v John Delaney>[LAUGHTER]
<v John Delaney>[笑声]

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:28.890
<v John Delaney>But I think what makes it best is it's different, and I can get it done.
<v John Delaney>但我认为最棒的是它是不同的，我可以完成它。

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:38.070
<v Zoe Chace>Delaney's moderate. He's not into Medicare for All. He's not into the Green New Deal. Even his climate change plan is all wrapped up in red, white, and blue bunting.
<v Zoe Chace>德莱尼是个温和派。他不赞成“全民医保”。他不赞成“绿色新政”。即使他的气候变化计划也包裹在红白蓝三色旗中。

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:44.200
<v John Delaney>Because we're solving the problem the old-fashioned American way by inventing and building.
<v John Delaney>因为我们正在以老式的美国方式通过发明和建造来解决这个问题。

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:45.140
<v Man>Hear hear!
<v Man>说得好！

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:46.560
<v Man>[APPLAUSE]
<v Man>[掌声]

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:50.340
<v Zoe Chace>The room is packed, actually. People here are eating John Delaney up.
<v Zoe Chace>事实上，房间挤满了人。这里的人很吃约翰·德莱尼那一套。

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:51.520
<v Ira Glass>So it's working.
<v Ira Glass>所以这奏效了。

00:13:51.520 --> 00:14:07.710
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah. I came to see exactly this, one of these 1%-ers winning people over to their side. And Delaney is betting his chips on Iowa. He's got eight offices there. That's more than anyone else, as of a couple weeks ago, anyway. He's been there the most times.
<v Zoe Chace>是的。我来这里就是为了看这个，看其中一个1%的人如何赢得人们的支持。德莱尼把筹码压在了爱荷华州。他在那里有八个办事处。至少在几周前，这是人数最多的。他去那里的次数最多。

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:27.530
<v Zoe Chace>We ran into him in the airport on the way there. It was his 29th trip. He's doing the purist version of this play on the campaign trail, a Jimmy Carter play. It's basically get a huge surge by doing great in the Iowa caucuses, the first in the nation. And the way you do that is voter by voter. That's what he's doing out here.
<v Zoe Chace>我们在去那里的路上在机场遇到了他。这是他的第29次行程。他正在竞选活动中进行这种玩法的纯粹版本，吉米·卡特的玩法。基本上是通过在全国第一个爱荷华州党团会议上表现出色来获得巨大的激增。而你做到这一点的方法就是逐个击破选民。他正在这里做这件事。

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:52.090
<v Dave Weigel>That's what Carter did. Carter was the first candidate who ever did that. They didn't have a caucus before, or they didn't have a caucus people paid attention to before. He lived here. Came in famously second to uncommitted. It was like uncommitted, 30, Carter, 25 or something. And people were like, oh, he beat all the much more famous senators. Who is this guy? So that was more than 40 years ago. But it's still like--
<v Dave Weigel>那就是卡特做的事情。卡特是第一个那样做的候选人。他们以前没有党团会议，或者他们没有关注党团会议。他住在这里。著名地排在“不承诺”之后。就像“不承诺”30%，卡特25%之类的。人们当时就想，哦，他击败了所有更著名的参议员。这个家伙是谁？那已经是40多年前的事了。但它仍然像——

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:55.320
<v Zoe Chace>So everybody's doing this Carter play. That's how you could think about it.
<v Zoe Chace>所以每个人都在玩这个卡特游戏。这就是你可以考虑的方式。

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:56.550
<v Dave Weigel>A lot of them.
<v Dave Weigel>他们中的许多人。

00:14:56.550 --> 00:15:10.890
<v Zoe Chace>I talked to one voter. He liked this meeting so much, he said he might caucus for Delaney. He might. He's still shopping around. But the number of people shopping has dropped since the first debate. And the window's closing for the 1%-ers.
<v Zoe Chace>我和一位选民谈过。他非常喜欢这次会议，他说他可能会在党团会议上投票给德莱尼。他可能会。他还在货比三家。但自第一次辩论以来，货比三家的人数有所下降。对于这些1%的人来说，机会之窗正在关闭。

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:19.720
<v Ira Glass>The debates-- they are probably the biggest chance that any of these underdogs get to rise out of the underdog pack and come barking to the forefront.
<v Ira Glass>辩论——这可能是任何这些弱势群体候选人从弱势群体中崛起并发出声音来到前台的最大机会。

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:23.010
<v Zoe Chace>The next one's at the end of the month. There has been one so far, of course.
<v Zoe Chace>下一次在月底。当然，目前已经进行了一次。

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:39.770
<v Ira Glass>And two days before the first debate, the team for one of the candidates, as you know, Zoe, let me watch a full day of debate prep. And by sheer luck, it was the candidate that most of the media declared the breakout star of the first night of the debate, Julián Castro. Zoe, you ready to hear the story?
<v Ira Glass>在第一次辩论前两天，其中一位候选人的团队，正如你所知，佐伊，让我看了一整天的辩论准备。碰巧，这位候选人就是大多数媒体宣布的辩论第一晚的黑马，胡利安·卡斯特罗。佐伊，你准备好听这个故事了吗？

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:40.810
<v Zoe Chace>Obviously.
<v Zoe Chace>当然。

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:44.880
<v Ira Glass>OK. Here is the team that got him to victory.
<v Ira Glass>好的。这就是带领他走向胜利的团队。

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:48.570
<v Ira Glass>OK, so the three of you, have you ever done debate prep before?
<v Ira Glass>好的，你们三个，你们以前做过辩论准备吗？

00:15:48.570 --> 00:15:49.120
<v Maya Rupert>Nope.
<v Maya Rupert>没有。

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:49.780
<v Jen Fiore>No.
<v Jen Fiore>没有。

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:50.630
<v Sawyer Hackett>No.
<v Sawyer Hackett>没有。

00:15:50.630 --> 00:16:15.610
<v Ira Glass>Meet Maya Rupert, Jen Fiore, and Sawyer Hackett, the campaign manager, communications advisor, and national press secretary for Julián Castro. Castro was mayor of San Antonio, then ran the Department of Housing and Urban Development under President Obama. They all worked for Castro there. If you add it up, they had done three full days of debate prep before this one, the one that I saw. And they were going to do one more after that. That is how important this is to them.
<v Ira Glass>来见见玛雅·鲁珀特、詹·菲奥里和索耶·哈克特，他们分别是胡利安·卡斯特罗的竞选经理、传播顾问和全国新闻秘书。卡斯特罗曾任圣安东尼奥市市长，后来在奥巴马总统领导下管理住房和城市发展部。他们都在那里为卡斯特罗工作。如果你算一下，他们在这次辩论之前已经进行了三整天的辩论准备，就是我看到的那次。之后他们还要再做一次。这对他们来说有多重要。

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:18.880
<v Derek Eadon>All right, so this is what we've got on the agenda for this morning.
<v Derek Eadon>好的，这就是我们今天早上议程上的内容。

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:38.660
<v Ira Glass>We're in a hotel conference room in Miami. All over the walls are these big pages torn from flip charts, each with a different topic and then three or four possible talking points for the candidate to use in the debate that they worked out in previous sessions. The guy talking is deputy campaign manager, Derek Eadon, who is running these sessions. And he's up in front by the easel with the flip chart.
<v Ira Glass>我们在迈阿密的酒店会议室。墙上到处都是从翻页挂图上撕下来的大纸张，每一张都有不同的主题，然后有三到四个可能的谈话要点供候选人在辩论中使用，这些都是他们在之前的会议中确定的。正在说话的是副竞选经理德里克·伊顿，他负责这些会议。他站在画架旁的翻页挂图前。

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:53.520
<v Ira Glass>And the candidate is standing at one of five podiums in front of the room. He's in jeans and a white dress shirt, sleeves rolled up, immaculately clean, white Adidas, iced tea, which, by the way, he told the New York Times is his comfort food on the road.
<v Ira Glass>候选人站在房间前的五个讲台之一处。他穿着牛仔裤和白色礼服衬衫，袖子卷起来，一尘不染，穿着白色阿迪达斯，喝着冰茶，顺便说一下，他告诉《纽约时报》，这是他在路上的慰藉食物。

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:56.290
<v Derek Eadon>And then we want to talk through a little bit of closing statement.
<v Derek Eadon>然后我们要讨论一点结束语。

00:16:56.290 --> 00:17:15.180
<v Ira Glass>Castro's staff, I should say, is deeply aware that most voters still do not know who Julián Castro is. And they told me that success in the first debate, to them, would be something very basic-- that voters would notice Castro, that he's in the race. Here's Jen Fiore, his senior communications advisor.
<v Ira Glass>我应该说，卡斯特罗的员工非常清楚，大多数选民仍然不知道胡利安·卡斯特罗是谁。他们告诉我，他们认为第一次辩论的成功将是非常基本的——选民会注意到卡斯特罗，他正在竞选中。这是他的高级传播顾问詹·菲奥里的说法。

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:27.010
<v Jen Fiore>Maybe it changes their math, right? Maybe they were supporting one person. They thought, oh, but wait. But wait. I heard a lot of interesting stuff from Julián Castro, and I've got to shift my math.
<v Jen Fiore>也许这改变了他们的想法，对吧？也许他们支持某个人。他们心想，哦，但是等等。但是等等。我从胡利安·卡斯特罗那里听到了很多有趣的事情，我必须改变我的想法。

00:17:27.010 --> 00:17:44.040
<v Ira Glass>One of the flip charts on the wall was, "Top Five Takeaways," meaning the top five things that they want voters to take away from this debate. They read, "I like him," "he won," "game changer," "can handle his own," and "win/beat Trump."
<v Ira Glass>墙上的翻页挂图之一是“前五大收获”，意思是他们希望选民从这次辩论中获得的前五件事。上面写着：“我喜欢他”、“他赢了”、“游戏规则改变者”、“能独当一面”和“赢/打败特朗普”。

00:17:44.040 --> 00:18:16.720
<v Ira Glass>So OK. How do you get voters to change the math? If you think about it, the debate means 10 candidates standing on stage for 120 minutes, two hours. So that's 10 minutes per candidate. But you take away commercial breaks and questions. What it leaves, the average talking time for each candidate is actually eight minutes-- eight minutes to implant yourselves into the brains of millions of people. Eight minutes-- though that is just the average. Not everybody gets that much, which is actually the thing that Castro is most afraid of going into the debate. He says they've studied these moderators.
<v Ira Glass>好的。你怎么让选民改变想法？如果你想一想，辩论意味着10位候选人站在台上120分钟，两个小时。所以每位候选人有10分钟。但除去广告时间和提问。剩下的，每位候选人的平均谈话时间实际上是8分钟——8分钟，将你们自己植入数百万人的大脑中。8分钟——虽然那只是平均值。不是每个人都能得到那么多，这实际上是卡斯特罗在辩论前最担心的事情。他说他们研究了这些主持人。

00:18:16.720 --> 00:18:35.000
<v Julian Castro>And they don't have a track record of actually enforcing time very well. So you've got to be mindful. OK, if they're going to let people go, the worst case scenario is not that I am flat, it's that I don't get any time, whatsoever. Because people got into a skirmish, and other people butted in. And all of a sudden, the time's gone.
<v Julian Castro>他们并没有很好地执行时间的记录。所以你必须小心。好的，如果他们要让人们继续说，最坏的情况不是我表现平平，而是我根本没得到任何时间。因为人们陷入了争吵，其他人插话了。突然间，时间没了。

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:54.770
<v Ira Glass>Some candidates just end up getting five minutes. So you have limited time, and you have no idea what they're going to ask you about. And so you have to prepare perfect, pithy answers for every imaginable question. And what perfect means-- Julián's staff wants him to work three specific elements into each of those answers if he can.
<v Ira Glass>有些候选人最终只能得到五分钟。所以你的时间有限，而且你根本不知道他们会问你什么。所以你必须为每一个可以想象的问题准备完美、简练的答案。什么是完美——胡利安的员工希望他如果可以的话，在每一个答案中加入三个具体要素。

00:18:54.770 --> 00:19:19.610
<v Ira Glass>They want some of his personal story-- raised by a single mother, working class neighborhood, that kind of thing. They want him to talk about the stuff that he has done in previous jobs in government-- prove that he's capable. And of course, they want him to talk about what he's going to do next if he gets the job of president. All three things in each answer. So for instance, when they're hammering out an answer about climate and the Green New Deal, Castro, he starts pitching policy ideas.
<v Ira Glass>他们想要他个人故事的一些内容——由单亲妈妈抚养长大，工人阶级社区，诸如此类。他们想要他谈论他在之前政府工作中做过的事情——证明他有能力。当然，他们想要他谈论如果他得到总统这份工作，他下一步要做什么。每个答案中都有这三件事。所以例如，当他们在敲定关于气候和绿色新政的答案时，卡斯特罗，他开始推销政策想法。

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:29.090
<v Julian Castro>I think here, we want to stress-- we're going to lead on combating climate change, get to net zero, create jobs in the new energy--
<v Julian Castro>我想在这里，我们要强调——我们将引领应对气候变化，实现净零排放，在新能源领域创造就业——

00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:31.850
<v Ira Glass>Then Jen jumps in. Talk more about your experience.
<v Ira Glass>然后詹插话了。多谈谈你的经验。

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:46.160
<v Jen Fiore>Right, but not a lot of an impact. Maybe nobody on the stage, other than maybe Governor Inslee, has actually done the work to help people recover the way that you have, right, as HUD secretary. I remember the trip that you took to Louisiana after that horrendous flood, and--
<v Jen Fiore>对，但没有太大的影响。可能台上除了英斯利州长之外，没有人真正像你作为住房部长那样做过帮助人们恢复的工作，对吧？我记得你在那场可怕的洪水后去路易斯安那州的旅行，而且——

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:52.640
<v Ira Glass>Talk about what you've done on climate change, she urges him, which he then incorporates into his next run-through of the answer.
<v Ira Glass>谈谈你在气候变化方面所做的事情，她敦促他，然后他将此整合到他接下来的答案重演中。

00:19:52.640 --> 00:20:03.740
<v Julian Castro>As Housing Secretary, I worked to make sure that communities could rebuild from natural disasters in a more sustainable way. And as president, the first thing that I would do--
<v Julian Castro>作为住房部长，我致力于确保社区能以更可持续的方式从自然灾害中重建。而作为总统，我做的第一件事就是——

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:21.050
<v Ira Glass>Notice the pivot there from his previous experience to his plans for the future? What makes all this so tricky is that under the rules of the debate, he has to finish his answer in just 60 seconds. To figure out how to fit everything in, basically, Castro answers the same question over--
<v Ira Glass>注意从他过去的经验到他未来计划的过渡了吗？让这一切如此棘手的是，根据辩论规则，他必须在60秒内完成回答。为了找出如何塞进所有内容，基本上，卡斯特罗一次又一次地回答同一个问题——

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:23.390
<v Julian Castro>I grew up with a grandmother that had diabetes.
<v Julian Castro>我是在一个有糖尿病的祖母身边长大的。

00:20:23.390 --> 00:20:24.140
<v Ira Glass>--and over--
<v Ira Glass>——一次又——

00:20:24.140 --> 00:20:27.470
<v Julian Castro>I grew up with a grandmother who had diabetes.
<v Julian Castro>我是在一个有糖尿病的祖母身边长大的。

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:28.310
<v Ira Glass>--and over--
<v Ira Glass>——一次又——

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:31.250
<v Julian Castro>I grew up with a grandmother who had diabetes.
<v Julian Castro>我是在一个有糖尿病的祖母身边长大的。

