<v Emanuele Berry>打击我的是每个人都想要他的注意力和时间。媒体在他前面。他们的摄像机对准了他。他有一个告诉他去哪里的助手。然后他领导着这群人，在走路时鼓掌欢呼。这真的太多了，一次性。

00:35:06.310 --> 00:35:11.780
<v Crowd>Give me a Y! Y! [INAUDIBLE] Give me a C! C!
<v Crowd>给我一个Y！Y！[听不清] 给我一个C！C！

00:35:11.780 --> 00:35:16.820
<v Emanuele Berry>So he's clapping. And then in the middle of all this, someone else walks up to him.
<v Emanuele Berry>所以他在鼓掌。然后在这一切之中，另一个人走近他。

00:35:16.820 --> 00:35:20.390
<v Cory Booker>God bless you, brother. God bless you, brother. [LAUGHS]
<v Cory Booker>上帝保佑你，兄弟。上帝保佑你，兄弟。[笑声]

00:35:20.390 --> 00:35:24.530
<v Emanuele Berry>It's Jesse Jackson, the civil rights icon, former presidential candidate.
<v Emanuele Berry>那是杰西·杰克逊，民权偶像，前总统候选人。

00:35:24.530 --> 00:35:25.190
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:35:25.190 --> 00:35:39.830
<v Emanuele Berry>Jesse Jackson is someone who Booker actually talks about sometimes as the first presidential candidate that he ever voted for. And he's just standing next to him. He just pops out of nowhere, and then he's at Booker's side. We're in South Carolina, remember? Jesse Jackson--
<v Emanuele Berry>杰西·杰克逊有时是布克提到的是他投过票的第一位总统候选人。他就站在他旁边。他不知从哪冒出来，然后他就在布克身边了。我们是在南卡罗来纳州，记住？杰西·杰克逊——

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:42.320
<v Ira Glass>Huge black vote-- every candidate's got to try to get it.
<v Ira Glass>巨大的黑人选票——每一位候选人都必须努力去争取。

00:35:42.320 --> 00:36:02.240
<v Emanuele Berry>Yeah. And I mean, Jesse Jackson's not the worst person to stand next to. He's from South Carolina. People, they remember when he ran for president. So you sort of have these two black men in dark suits, one of them who tried to be president and the other one who's trying to do so now, parading down the street together.
<v Emanuele Berry>是的。我的意思是，杰西·杰克逊站在旁边不是最糟糕的人。他来自南卡罗来纳州。人们，他们记得他竞选总统的时候。所以你有一种这两个穿深色西装的黑人男人的感觉，其中一个人试图成为总统，另一个人现在正在努力这样做，一起走在街上。

00:36:02.240 --> 00:36:04.330
<v Cory Booker>Yeah, I'm getting ready to go and speak now.
<v Cory Booker>是的，我现在正准备去演讲。

00:36:04.330 --> 00:36:21.300
<v Emanuele Berry>Jackson is hard to hear. He has Parkinson's. He speaks slow and stumbly, and I don't have a mic on him. Booker seems pretty chummy. He basically says, yes, we need to talk. We need to have a conversation, but not now.
<v Emanuele Berry>杰克逊很难听清楚。他患有帕金森症。他说话慢且迟钝，我没有麦克风对他。布克看起来很亲热。他基本上说，是的，我们需要谈谈。我们需要交谈，但现在不行。

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:32.850
<v Cory Booker>I'll try to reach out on the number I have for you, but I want to talk to you when we're not-- all this craziness going around. So maybe tomorrow after church? Are you still going to be down here?
<v Cory Booker>我会尝试用我有的号码联系你，但我想在我们不是——所有这些疯狂发生时和你谈话。所以也许明天教堂之后？你还会在这里吗？

00:36:32.850 --> 00:36:34.040
<v Jesse Jackson>In Chicago.
<v Jesse Jackson>在芝加哥。

00:36:34.040 --> 00:36:34.310
<v Cory Booker>Oh.
<v Cory Booker>哦。

00:36:34.310 --> 00:36:52.850
<v Emanuele Berry>We often see pictures like this, right? Like leaders in suits and conversation in this public space. And I don't know. I like to think of the conversations they're having are about these great and epic things. But maybe they're actually just scheduling phone calls. [LAUGHS]
<v Emanuele Berry>我们经常看到这样的照片，对吧？就像西装革履的领导人在公共空间交谈。我不知道。我喜欢认为他们进行的对话是关于这些伟大和史诗般的事情。但也许他们实际上只是在安排电话。[笑声]

00:36:52.850 --> 00:37:08.440
<v Cory Booker>So let's just talk on the phone, OK? We need to have a heart-to-heart sooner or later. But look, you are doing it right now. You're continuing to keep people's eyes on justice. Thank you. Love you.
<v Cory Booker>所以让我们在电话里谈吧，好吗？我们需要迟早有一次掏心窝子的谈话。但听着，你现在正在做这件事。你继续让人们的眼睛盯着正义。谢谢。爱你。

00:37:08.440 --> 00:37:14.280
<v Emanuele Berry>You can hear that Corey says love you to Jackson. I'm not sure if he says it back. I just can't hear it.
<v Emanuele Berry>你可以听到科里对杰克逊说爱你。我不确定他是否回应了。我只是听不到。

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:18.550
<v Ira Glass>Or Jackson might just be like in Star Wars. I love you. I know.
<v Ira Glass>或者杰克逊可能就像在星球大战里一样。我爱你。我知道。

00:37:18.550 --> 00:37:20.160
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHTER]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:20.800
<v Emanuele Berry>I don't think so.
<v Emanuele Berry>我不这么认为。

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:22.230
<v Ira Glass>Jackson leaves him hanging.
<v Ira Glass>杰克逊让他悬着。

00:37:22.230 --> 00:37:26.500
<v Emanuele Berry>I don't think so. I imagine that he returned the expression.
<v Emanuele Berry>我不这么认为。我想象他回应了这个表达。

00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:32.340
<v Ira Glass>We don't know for sure, though.
<v Ira Glass>不过我们不确定。

00:37:32.340 --> 00:37:38.220
<v Emanuele Berry>And that was only five minutes and 47 seconds of his day. And it was so intense.
<v Emanuele Berry>那只是他一天中5分47秒的内容。而且太激烈了。

00:37:38.220 --> 00:37:38.760
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:55.700
<v Emanuele Berry>And here's what else he did that day. He had a faith breakfast, barbershop meet-and-greet, Planned Parenthood forum, convention center speech, and then another meet-and-greet. Also, he's a senator. That was just a moment of his day. And I can't imagine having an entire day like that.
<v Emanuele Berry>这就是他那天做的其他事情。他有一个信仰早餐、理发店见面会、计划生育论坛、会议中心演讲，然后是另一个见面会。此外，他还是位参议员。那只是他一天的瞬间。我无法想象过那样的一整天。

00:37:55.700 --> 00:37:58.770
<v Ira Glass>Ugh, it's so much work, and he's not even a frontrunner.
<v Ira Glass>呃，这工作量太大了，而且他甚至不是领跑者。

00:37:58.770 --> 00:38:09.400
<v Emanuele Berry>It's so much work just to be there.
<v Emanuele Berry>光是站在那里就需要做这么多工作。

00:38:09.400 --> 00:38:11.700
<v Ira Glass>So now I'm joined in the studio by Ben Calhoun. Hey there.
<v Ira Glass>所以现在本·卡尔霍恩加入我的录音室了。嘿。

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:12.480
<v Ben Calhoun>Hey.
<v Ben Calhoun>嘿。

00:38:12.480 --> 00:38:22.140
<v Ira Glass>So you went out with one of the 20 or so underdogs in the race, Andrew Yang. And Yang, I think, is interesting because he's actually managed to get a lot more attention than most of the underdogs.
<v Ira Glass>所以你和这场竞选中的20个左右弱势群体候选人之一，杨安泽一起出去了。杨，我觉得很有趣，因为他实际上比大多数弱势候选人设法获得了更多的关注。

00:38:22.140 --> 00:38:37.350
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah. He's been doing things like getting on cable shows, profiles in Vanity Fair, Washington Post. And so now he's in this position where he's just trying to fan like whatever little flames he's kindled up until this point.
<v Ben Calhoun>是的。他一直在做一些事情，比如上电视节目，在《名利场》、《华盛顿邮报》上亮相。所以现在他处于这种位置，他只是试图煽动他到目前为止点燃的任何小火苗。

00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:46.590
<v Ira Glass>And he's gotten that attention because, well, if you know who this guy is, he's this tech guy, former businessman, who organized his whole campaign around this one idea.
<v Ira Glass>他得到这种关注是因为，嗯，如果你知道这家伙是谁，他是个科技大亨，前商人，他围绕这个想法组织了他的整个竞选。

00:38:46.590 --> 00:39:08.220
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah, and the idea is that we should give every working age American $1,000 a month. Because what Yang says is he says this whole country is in this big crisis where workers are being replaced by automation and technology. And he thinks that when Democrats usually talk about this, their solutions are weak sauce. He says job retraining programs--
<v Ben Calhoun>是的，这个想法是我们应该给每个工作年龄的美国人每月1,000美元。因为杨所说的是，他说整个国家正处于一场巨大的危机中，工人正在被自动化和技术所取代。他认为当民主党人通常谈论这个时，他们的解决方案很软弱。他说工作再培训计划——

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:09.840
<v Ira Glass>The Democrats talk about that a lot.
<v Ira Glass>民主党人经常谈论那个。

00:39:09.840 --> 00:39:18.270
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah, job retraining. And he says the studies show that they don't work for most people. And most people kind of feel that in their gut.
<v Ben Calhoun>是的，工作再培训。他说研究表明它们对大多数人不起作用。大多数人从直觉上感觉得到。

00:39:18.270 --> 00:39:47.640
<v Andrew Yang>Because it is bull [BLEEP]. And national politicians will talk about retraining until they're blue in the face. They love it, [LAUGHS] because they're far from the group of people that are getting displaced. But if you get close-- I was at a truck stop here in Iowa, Iowa 80. And you walk around there, talking about retraining to those guys, you'll probably get a fist to the face. [LAUGHS] It's irresponsible to talk about that as a mass solution.
<v Andrew Yang>因为这是废话。全国政客们会谈论再培训直到他们面红耳赤。他们喜欢它，[笑声]因为他们离被取代的那群人很远。但如果你接近——我在爱荷华州的一个卡车停靠站，爱荷华80。你在那里走动，和那些家伙谈论再培训，你可能会脸部挨上一拳。[笑声] 把这作为一种群众性解决方案来谈论是不负责任的。

00:39:47.640 --> 00:40:11.620
<v Ben Calhoun>So $1,000 a month, which Yang admits, it's not enough for somebody to live on, but he says it's enough to keep somebody afloat. The name for that is Universal Basic Income, UBI. Yang's calling his version of this the Freedom Dividend. But so I went to go see him. He's going to demonstrate how this would work by actually giving somebody $1,000 a month, no strings attached.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以每月1,000美元，杨承认，这不足以让某人生活，但他说这足以让某人维持生存。这叫做全民基本收入，UBI。杨称他的这个版本为“自由红利”。但所以我去看了他。他要演示这是如何工作的，通过实际给某人每月1,000美元，没有附加条件。

00:40:11.620 --> 00:40:12.540
<v Ira Glass>For a year?
<v Ira Glass>一年？

00:40:12.540 --> 00:40:14.860
<v Ben Calhoun>A whole year out of his pocket.
<v Ben Calhoun>自掏腰包整整一年。

00:40:14.860 --> 00:40:17.040
<v Ira Glass>OK. So tell what happened.
<v Ira Glass>好的。所以告诉我发生了什么。

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:27.210
<v Ben Calhoun>So the night before this thing, I'm in the car with Yang and his campaign. And I've been recording them all day. Now it's like 9:30 PM. Everybody's pretty tired.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以这件事的前一天晚上，我和杨以及他的竞选团队在车里。我已经录了他们一整天。现在是晚上9:30。每个人都相当累。

00:40:27.210 --> 00:40:31.290
<v Staffer>So it's an hour and a half from here. We need to be somewhere at 10:45, so we need to leave--
<v Staffer>所以离这里有一个半小时。我们需要在10:45出现在某个地方，所以我们需要离开——

00:40:31.290 --> 00:40:46.760
<v Ben Calhoun>And they're talking about this UBI event. Yang's like, I'm so psyched. But then right away, there's this logistical problem. To give away $1,000, you need to hand people something. And he doesn't have $1,000 on him.
<v Ben Calhoun>他们在谈论这个UBI活动。杨说，我太兴奋了。但随即，出现了一个物流问题。为了送出1,000美元，你需要交给人们什么东西。而且他身上没有1,000美元。

00:40:46.760 --> 00:40:50.190
<v Andrew Yang>What they want to do is like-- I've gotta stop by an ATM and--
<v Andrew Yang>他们想要做的是像——我得顺便去个ATM和——

00:40:50.190 --> 00:41:03.040
<v Ben Calhoun>OK, it's pretty much impossible to hear. I'm way in the back. I'm behind Yang's campaign manager, Zach Graumann, and Yang. They're up in front. So I'm just going to tell you how a lot of this dialogue goes.
<v Ben Calhoun>好的，几乎不可能听清。我在后面。我在杨的竞选经理扎克·格劳曼和杨后面。他们在前面。所以我只是要告诉你很多对话是怎么进行的。

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:24.080
<v Ben Calhoun>In that tape there, Yang says, I need to stop by an ATM and get a lot of cash, right? Because Ed McMahon doesn't show up at your house and just tell you you've won publisher's clearing house. That would be lame. You take a photo with the big check. So Yang's like, I got to get out a bunch of cash, to which his campaign manager says, well--
<v Ben Calhoun>在那盘带子里，杨说，我需要顺便去个ATM取很多现金，对吧？因为埃德·麦克马洪不会出现在你家门口就告诉你你赢了出版商清算所。那太蹩脚了。你拿着大支票拍照。所以杨说，我得取出一大堆现金，他的竞选经理说，嗯——

00:41:24.080 --> 00:41:27.830
<v Zach Graumann>So you cannot give cash. We need to think about this.
<v Zach Graumann>所以你不能给现金。我们需要考虑一下。

00:41:27.830 --> 00:41:53.040
<v Ben Calhoun>"So you cannot give cash. We need to think about this," he says. And he gives this big sigh. Because shockingly, the Federal Elections Commission has a problem with a candidate for president giving a voter $12,000 in cash. Go figure. But also apparently, it's fine if that money comes in the form of a check. Again, go figure. Questions start flying. Does anybody have a check? Hey, what about Don?
<v Ben Calhoun>“所以你不能给现金。我们需要考虑一下，”他说。他发出了这声沉重的叹息。因为令人震惊的是，联邦选举委员会对于总统候选人给选民12,000美元现金有问题。去想吧。但同样显然，如果那笔钱以支票形式出现，那是可以的。再说一次，去想吧。问题开始飞来了。谁有支票？嘿，唐怎么样？

00:41:53.040 --> 00:41:56.310
<v Staffer 1>Don's son would probably have a check, right? Don's son [INAUDIBLE].
<v Staffer 1>唐的儿子应该有支票，对吧？唐的儿子[听不清]。

00:41:56.310 --> 00:41:58.170
<v Staffer 2>I can go to a bank and get a check, right?
<v Staffer 2>我可以去银行拿张支票，对吧？

00:41:58.170 --> 00:42:14.210
<v Ben Calhoun>Yang, meantime, gets out his wallet and starts counting how much cash he has on him. $270, it turns out. And then he starts asking how much ATM limits are. How about a cashier's check, someone says, at a Walgreens on Sunday morning?
<v Ben Calhoun>杨，同时，拿出他的钱包开始数他身上有多少现金。结果是270美元。然后他开始问ATM限额是多少。有人说，周日早上在沃尔格林拿张现金支票怎么样？

00:42:14.210 --> 00:42:16.400
<v Staffer 1>Getting to a Walgreens before 9:00 AM tomorrow.
<v Staffer 1>明天早上9点前去沃尔格林。

00:42:16.400 --> 00:42:17.670
<v Staffer 2>That's not impossible.
<v Staffer 2>那不是不可能。

00:42:17.670 --> 00:42:45.630
<v Ben Calhoun>I'm in a car with a candidate for the presidency of the United States of America who's trying to sell the marquee idea of his campaign, the concept he thinks will save America. And the question of the moment is, can you get a cashier's check at a Walgreens in Des Moines on Sunday morning before 9:00 AM?
<v Ben Calhoun>我正坐在美国总统候选人的车里，他正试图推销他竞选的核心想法，那个他认为能拯救美国的概念。而当下的问题是，你能在周日早上9点前在得梅因的沃尔格林拿到现金支票吗？

00:42:45.630 --> 00:43:02.040
<v Ben Calhoun>The next day, we go to the house of the Freedom Dividend recipients, Kyle and Pam Christiansen. At this point, the campaign hasn't told them they're getting the money, just that they're being considered as finalists. Yang isn't with us. He's waiting to make a surprise entrance.
<v Ben Calhoun>第二天，我们去了自由红利接受者凯尔和帕姆·克里斯蒂安森的家。此时，竞选团队还没告诉他们他们要拿钱，只是说他们正在被考虑作为决赛选手。杨没和我们在一起。他正在等待做一个惊喜的进入。

00:43:02.040 --> 00:43:24.450
<v Ben Calhoun>His staff was not able to get a cashier's check. So instead, Yang has a grand in $20 bills. That means that, legally, the campaign is going to have to hand the Christiansens the money for this photo op, but then take it back afterwards and promise to mail them a check, which seems really pretty tacky to me. But everyone with the Yang campaign is like, we'll figure it out.
<v Ben Calhoun>他的员工没能拿到现金支票。所以相反，杨有1,000美元的20美元钞票。这意味着，在法律上，竞选团队将不得不为这次拍照把钱交给克里斯蒂安森一家，但之后得把钱收回去，并承诺给他们寄一张支票，这对我来说看起来真的很俗气。但杨竞选团队的每个人都说，我们会搞定的。

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:25.920
<v Zach Graumann>Hi. I'm Zach.
<v Zach Graumann>嗨。我是扎克。

00:43:25.920 --> 00:43:26.460
<v Pam Christiansen>I'm Pam.
<v Pam Christiansen>我是帕姆。

00:43:26.460 --> 00:43:29.190
<v Zach Graumann>Nice to meet you in person, Pam. How are you?
<v Zach Graumann>很高兴见到你，帕姆。你好吗？

00:43:29.190 --> 00:43:48.060
<v Ben Calhoun>The campaign, I've got to say, picked a really lovely family to get this. Kyle, the son, is 41. He'd applied for this Freedom Dividend on behalf of his mom. About four years ago, the family lost Kyle's dad, Merle, to brain cancer. They seemed like such a tight, loving family.
<v Ben Calhoun>我必须说，竞选团队挑选了一个非常可爱的家庭来获得这个。凯尔，儿子，41岁。他代他母亲申请了这个自由红利。大约四年前，这个家庭失去了凯尔的爸爸，默尔，死于脑癌。他们看起来是一个如此紧密、有爱的家庭。

00:43:48.060 --> 00:44:02.970
<v Ben Calhoun>Merle was a musician. And in their house, every Wednesday, it was music night. They'd play records-- Kiss, Black Sabbath. Merle would play drums, and the kids would dance around, which is how, I suppose, Kyle ended up being a musician, too.
<v Ben Calhoun>默尔是一名音乐家。在他们家里，每周三是音乐之夜。他们会放唱片——KISS，黑色安息日。默尔会打鼓，孩子们会跳舞，我想，这就是凯尔最终也成为音乐家的原因。

00:44:02.970 --> 00:44:21.090
<v Ben Calhoun>Anyway, about a year after Merle died, Pam was also diagnosed with cancer. The day she was diagnosed-- her birthday-- her boss called her to tell her he was firing her because she'd had to miss too much work. Pam worked as an aide for disabled adults, which she said was hard, but she loved it.
<v Ben Calhoun>总之，默尔去世大约一年后，帕姆也被诊断出癌症。她被诊断出的那天——她的生日——她的老板打电话告诉她他要解雇她，因为她不得不缺席太多工作。帕姆曾是一名残疾成年人的助手，她说这很难，但她喜欢它。

00:44:21.090 --> 00:44:39.930
<v Ben Calhoun>After that, Kyle dedicated himself to taking care of Pam. And Pam, now in remission, still has a hard time getting around. Kyle's been piecing together work-- auto repair, computer repair, music work when he can get it. But it's hard making sure he's there for his mom.
<v Ben Calhoun>那之后，凯尔致力于照顾帕姆。帕姆现在处于缓解期，仍然很难四处走动。凯尔一直在拼凑工作——汽车维修、电脑维修、音乐工作。但确保他在他妈妈身边很难。

00:44:39.930 --> 00:44:55.460
<v Ben Calhoun>The monthly bills are about $1,300, and they barely get covered. So $1,000 would mean a lot. Kyle, though, he says he'd like to see his mom spend at least some of this imaginary money, even just a little, on something genuinely frivolous.
<v Ben Calhoun>每月的账单大约是1,300美元，几乎无法支付。所以1,000美元意义重大。凯尔，不过，他说他想看到他妈妈在这笔想象中的钱里至少花一些，即使是一点点，在真正轻浮的事情上。

00:44:55.460 --> 00:45:01.830
<v Kyle Christiansen>Yeah, I'd just like to see her just go buy something because she wants to and not out of necessity.
<v Kyle Christiansen>是的，我只是想看到她因为想要而买点什么，而不是因为必要。

00:45:01.830 --> 00:45:04.360
<v Pam Christiansen>But there's priorities, too, so.
<v Pam Christiansen>但也有优先事项，所以。

00:45:04.360 --> 00:45:17.040
<v Ben Calhoun>Well, what are some of the things that-- I feel like you get sick, those bills pile up, you start to give things up. I mean, what are some of the things that you've given up in the last few years?
<v Ben Calhoun>嗯，有些什么事情——我觉得你生病了，那些账单堆积起来，你开始放弃东西。我的意思是，过去几年你放弃了什么？

00:45:17.040 --> 00:45:21.030
<v Kyle Christiansen>Are you talking about like what we have given up, or sold, or that kind of stuff?
<v Kyle Christiansen>你在谈论我们放弃了什么，或者卖掉了什么，或者那种事情吗？

00:45:21.030 --> 00:45:24.480
<v Zach Graumann>Yeah. Just like-- yeah, just since the money's been tighter.
<v Zach Graumann>是的。只是——是的，自从钱变得更紧之后。

00:45:24.480 --> 00:45:48.600
<v Kyle Christiansen>Yeah, I know for me, I used to have a full-blown recording studio here in town. And I've always kind of held on to all that equipment hoping to set one up somewhere. I've sold darn near all that equipment, and just kind of have bare bones, bare minimum. I sold my last two guitars that I could possibly part with last week. So-- oh my gosh, there's Andrew Yang himself.
<v Kyle Christiansen>是的，我知道对我来说，我曾经在城里有一个完整的录音室。我一直希望在这里设置一个。我把所有那些设备都卖掉了，只剩下光秃秃的，最低限度的。我上周卖掉了我最后两把还能卖的吉他。所以——噢，天哪，那是安德鲁·杨本人。

00:45:48.600 --> 00:45:50.340
<v Kyle Christiansen>[DOOR BELL RINGS]
<v Kyle Christiansen>[门铃响]

00:45:50.340 --> 00:45:52.770
<v Pam Christiansen>Oh, yes.
<v Pam Christiansen>哦，是的。

00:45:52.770 --> 00:46:10.810
<v Ben Calhoun>Kyle later told me, when he'd asked the campaign if Yang was coming, the staffer he was talking to on the phone hesitated and then said, no, in this way that Kyle figured Yang probably was coming. But he and Pam both act surprised. Andrew Yang offers to take off his shoes in the house.
<v Ben Calhoun>凯尔后来告诉我，当他问竞选团队杨是否要来时，他电话里交谈的那个员工犹豫了一下，然后说了不，以一种凯尔推测杨可能要来的方式。但他和帕姆都表现得大吃一惊。安德鲁·杨主动提出在屋里脱掉鞋子。

00:46:10.810 --> 00:46:12.330
<v Kyle Christiansen>Nope, nope, you're fine.
<v Kyle Christiansen>不，不，你没问题。

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:13.570
<v Andrew Yang>I am Asian, so.
<v Andrew Yang>我是亚裔，所以。

00:46:13.570 --> 00:46:15.050
<v Kyle Christiansen>No, come on.
<v Kyle Christiansen>不，别。

00:46:15.050 --> 00:46:16.030
<v Andrew Yang>I'll take them off.
<v Andrew Yang>我会脱掉它们的。

00:46:16.030 --> 00:46:17.410
<v Pam Christiansen>Hey, Mr. Yang. How are you?
<v Pam Christiansen>嘿，杨先生。你好吗？

00:46:17.410 --> 00:46:18.510
<v Andrew Yang>I'm doing great. You must be Pam.
<v Andrew Yang>我很好。你一定是帕姆。

00:46:18.510 --> 00:46:19.750
<v Pam Christiansen>Yes, I'm Pam.
<v Pam Christiansen>是的，我是帕姆。

00:46:19.750 --> 00:46:20.600
<v Andrew Yang>Thank you for having me here.
<v Andrew Yang>谢谢你邀请我。

00:46:20.600 --> 00:46:21.160
<v Pam Christiansen>Yes.
<v Pam Christiansen>是的。

00:46:21.160 --> 00:46:27.270
<v Andrew Yang>So I'm here to let you know that you will be receiving the Iowa Freedom Dividend starting July 1.
<v Andrew Yang>所以我在这里让你知道，你将从7月1日开始收到爱荷华州自由红利。

00:46:27.270 --> 00:46:28.180
<v Pam Christiansen>Oh!
<v Pam Christiansen>噢！

00:46:28.180 --> 00:46:29.270
<v Andrew Yang>So congratulations.
<v Andrew Yang>所以恭喜。

00:46:29.270 --> 00:46:31.320
<v Pam Christiansen>Thank you.
<v Pam Christiansen>谢谢。

00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:59.880
<v Ben Calhoun>If Yang did this for everyone, if he gave every adult $12,000 a year, it would cost the government $2 trillion, give or take. The current federal budget is $4 trillion. So we're talking about a massive realignment of the economy and redistribution of wealth. Yang has projections on how all this could be paid for, and they include some very optimistic assumptions, all for a theory that's never been tested on anywhere near the scale.
<v Ben Calhoun>如果杨对每个人都这样做，如果他给每个成年人每年12,000美元，那将花费政府2万亿美元左右。目前的联邦预算是4万亿美元。所以我们在谈论经济的重大重组和财富的重新分配。杨对于这一切如何支付有预测，它们包括一些非常乐观的假设，全部基于一个从未在任何接近那个规模的地方进行过测试的理论。

00:46:59.880 --> 00:47:15.550
<v Ben Calhoun>Yang starts to explain to the Christiansens how this is going to work for them. But mostly, he tells them how grateful he is to help them out, in a way that feels pretty nice, actually. And it kind of drains a little awkwardness out of a very manufactured situation.
<v Ben Calhoun>杨开始向克里斯蒂安森一家解释这将如何为他们工作。但大多时候，他告诉他们他有多感激能帮助他们，以一种感觉相当不错的方式。它从一个非常制造出来的情况下抽走了一点尴尬。

00:47:15.550 --> 00:47:34.790
<v Andrew Yang>So we need to do this for people all over the country, but starting here with you all to illustrate the fact that if people get some extra money in their hands, it's going to go to the things that we care about and value. I mean, it's an awesome opportunity for us. So thank you for making it possible. Give me a hug. No problem.
<v Andrew Yang>所以我们需要为全国各地的人们做这件事，但从你们所有人开始，以说明如果人们手里有一些额外的钱，它会流向我们关心和重视的事情。我的意思是，这对我们来说是一个很棒的机会。所以谢谢你让这成为可能。给我个拥抱。没问题。

00:47:34.790 --> 00:47:36.780
<v Pam Christiansen>Thank you. Thank you.
<v Pam Christiansen>谢谢。谢谢。

00:47:36.780 --> 00:47:40.020
<v Andrew Yang>You guys are very, very welcome.
<v Andrew Yang>你们非常非常受欢迎。

00:47:40.020 --> 00:48:13.020
<v Ben Calhoun>The next 45 minutes are completely usual and unusual. Yang genuinely asks Pam and Kyle about their situation-- Pam's neuropathy, her treatment. He talks again and again about how much he admires Kyle for taking care of his mom. Kyle talks about the one doctor's appointment he's missed in the last three years. They talk about music, how Kyle quit the metal band he was touring with because he loved performing, but he never had an interest in the drinking and drugs that went with it. They laugh about Yang's flag socks. He talks about picking them out.
<v Ben Calhoun>接下来的45分钟是完全平常和不平常的。杨真诚地问帕姆和凯尔关于他们的情况——帕姆的神经病变，她的治疗。他一再谈论他有多么钦佩凯尔照顾他妈妈。凯尔谈论他过去三年错过的一次医生的预约。他们谈论音乐，凯尔如何退出了他巡演的金属乐队，因为他喜欢表演，但他对伴随表演的饮酒和药物从不感兴趣。他们笑着杨的旗帜袜子。他谈论挑选它们。

00:48:13.020 --> 00:48:28.440
<v Ben Calhoun>Like so little of political campaigning, it's unrushed enough to feel regular. Until it's time to go and the surreal logistics of a political campaign break through this small bubble of normality. Yang has to hit the road.
<v Ben Calhoun>政治竞选活动中很少有这种情况，它足够从容，感觉很平常。直到该离开的时候，政治竞选的超现实物流打破了这个小的常态泡沫。杨必须上路了。

00:48:28.440 --> 00:48:50.040
<v Ben Calhoun>I feel like this is a weird and particular thing you see when politicians campaign. So many of their interactions are superficial and crassly abbreviated. And then sometimes, like in this room, they'll just drift into a space with some voters that feels intense, and authentic, and personal. And then poof, time to go.
<v Ben Calhoun>我觉得这是你看到政治家竞选时一种奇怪而特殊的事情。他们的许多互动是肤浅和粗俗简略的。然后有时，就像在这个房间里，他们会漂移到一些选民的空间里，感觉很激烈、真实和个人。然后砰，该走了。

00:48:50.040 --> 00:49:07.970
<v Ben Calhoun>Before Yang's got to leave, though, they have to pose for pictures. They have to pose with the cash, the cash they need, but won't be allowed to keep. After the pictures, they'll hand it back. For now, Yang pulls out this huge wad of twenties. Pam, with some struggle, stands up. And Yang hands each of them a stack.
<v Ben Calhoun>不过，在杨不得不离开之前，他们必须摆姿势拍照。他们必须和现金摆姿势，他们需要的现金，但不被允许保留。照片拍完后，他们会把它还回去。现在，杨拿出一大卷二十美元钞票。帕姆，带着一些挣扎，站了起来。杨把一叠交给他们每个人。

00:49:07.970 --> 00:49:09.090
<v Staffer>Yeah, can you fan it?
<v Staffer>是的，你能扇形展开它吗？

00:49:09.090 --> 00:49:10.800
<v Zach Graumann>Can you guys fan it?
<v Zach Graumann>你们能扇形展开它吗？

00:49:10.800 --> 00:49:14.750
<v Ben Calhoun>They fan out the money. And then they look for which lens to smile into.
<v Ben Calhoun>他们把钱扇形展开。然后他们寻找该对着哪个镜头微笑。

00:49:14.750 --> 00:49:15.640
<v Staffer>There you go.
<v Staffer>好了。

00:49:15.640 --> 00:49:17.790
<v Pam Christiansen>Split it in half. His pile's bigger than mine.
<v Pam Christiansen>对半分。他的那叠比我的大。

00:49:17.790 --> 00:49:20.210
<v Pam Christiansen>[LAUGHTER]
<v Pam Christiansen>[笑声]

00:49:20.210 --> 00:49:22.150
<v Staffer>Not that I'm counting, but--
<v Staffer>不是我在数，但是——

00:49:22.150 --> 00:49:24.570
<v Staffer>[CAMERA CLICKING]
<v Staffer>[相机咔哒声]

00:49:24.570 --> 00:49:26.340
<v Andrew Yang>And one, this is for Instagram.
<v Andrew Yang>还有一个，这是给Instagram的。

00:49:26.340 --> 00:49:27.780
<v Ira Glass>Ben Calhoun. Hey, Ben?
<v Ira Glass>本·卡尔霍恩。嘿，本？

00:49:27.780 --> 00:49:28.530
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah.
<v Ben Calhoun>是的。

00:49:28.530 --> 00:49:31.960
<v Ira Glass>I see in the poll numbers that he's still stuck at the bottom.
<v Ira Glass>我在民调数字中看到他还卡在底部。

00:49:31.960 --> 00:49:35.270
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah, and I mean, not the bottom, bottom.
<v Ben Calhoun>是的，我的意思是，不是底部中的底部。

00:49:35.270 --> 00:50:00.670
<v Ira Glass>It's so crazy that people aren't voting for this. It reminds me of-- I knew somebody who was an editor at Playboy magazine. Like once the internet hit and Maxim and all those magazines hit, and it was like Playboy was printing pornography, and people wouldn't buy the magazine. And that's what I feel like this is. It's like he's giving away $12,000 to everybody who will vote for him, and people still don't want it.
<v Ira Glass>人们不投这个票真是太疯狂了。这让我想起——我认识一个人，他是《花花公子》杂志的编辑。就像一旦互联网出现，马克西姆和所有那些杂志出现，就像《花花公子》在印刷色情作品，人们不会买那本杂志。我觉得这就是这种情况。就像他给所有投他票的人送去12,000美元，人们仍然不想要它。

00:50:00.670 --> 00:50:18.510
<v Ben Calhoun>Uh-huh. The family did say that they are going to caucus for him, if he's still around in January when the caucuses happen.
<v Ben Calhoun>嗯哼。那个家庭确实说，如果他在一月份党团会议时还在，他们会在党团会议上投他票。

00:50:18.510 --> 00:50:30.460
<v Dave Weigel>You have a town hall with the moderate guy who wants to beat up Medicare for All, or you have Gillibrand also at 1% with drag queens. So which of those is more interesting?
<v Dave Weigel>你有一个和想要打败全民医保的温和派家伙的市政厅会议，或者你还有支持1%的吉利布兰德和变装皇后。那么哪一个更有趣？

00:50:30.460 --> 00:50:31.690
<v Zoe Chace>Hi Ira, I'm back.
<v Zoe Chace>嘿艾拉，我回来了。

00:50:31.690 --> 00:50:32.670
<v Ira Glass>Hey there, Zoe Chace.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，佐伊·蔡斯。

00:50:32.670 --> 00:50:36.160
<v Zoe Chace>I'm taking you through Iowa with Dave Weigel, the Washington Post reporter.
<v Zoe Chace>我正带着你和《华盛顿邮报》记者戴夫·维格尔一起走访爱荷华州。

00:50:36.160 --> 00:50:39.550
<v Ira Glass>I know. I feel like you saw so much more than we heard at the top of the show.
<v Ira Glass>我知道。我觉得你看到的比我们在节目开头听到的多得多。

00:50:39.550 --> 00:50:46.840
<v Zoe Chace>It's kind of crazy the way Weigel wants to go to every single candidate event. That's impossible, because there are too many happening at the same time.
<v Zoe Chace>维格尔想要参加每一场候选人活动，这太疯狂了。那是不可能的，因为同时发生的事情太多了。

00:50:46.840 --> 00:50:49.270
<v Dave Weigel>It's not the hardest decision anyone's ever made. It's like--
<v Dave Weigel>这不是任何人做过的最艰难的决定。它就像——

00:50:49.270 --> 00:50:57.400
<v Zoe Chace>But there's this intense lesson that I think a lot of the reporters learned from 2016, which is that any candidate can surge.
<v Zoe Chace>但我觉得很多记者从2016年学到了一个强烈的教训，那就是任何候选人都可能激增。

00:50:57.400 --> 00:51:00.370
<v Ira Glass>Oh, you mean because Trump. Like, they didn't foresee that Trump could be the winner.
<v Ira Glass>哦，你是说因为特朗普。比如，他们没预见到特朗普会成为赢家。

00:51:00.370 --> 00:51:17.900
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, all the things that indicated who'd come out on top-- like think Jeb Bush, right? He was the front runner. He had all the money. None of those things turned out to matter. So this time around, it's like any event can be the beginning of someone's big run. We went to so many events in just two days.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，所有表明谁会脱颖而出的事情——想想杰布·布什，对吧？他是领跑者。他有所有的钱。结果这些事情都没关系。所以这一次，感觉任何活动都可能是某人伟大竞选的开始。我们在短短两天内参加了太多活动。

00:51:17.900 --> 00:51:21.310
<v Kirsten Gillibrand>She saved the mommy. She saved the daddy. She saved--
<v Kirsten Gillibrand>她拯救了妈妈。她拯救了爸爸。她拯救了——

00:51:21.310 --> 00:51:29.640
<v Zoe Chace>Kirsten Gillibrand, she wrote a kid's book. And she's reading it at the Y to just two small children, while a handful of people watch them.
<v Zoe Chace>克尔斯滕·吉利布兰德，她写了一本儿童书。她在基督教青年会给两个小孩读它，而只有少数几个人看着他们。

00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:31.300
<v Ira Glass>Wait, the senator wrote a kid's book?
<v Ira Glass>等等，参议员写了一本儿童书？

00:51:31.300 --> 00:51:34.650
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, she wrote a book for kids, but not that many kids showed up to hear it.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，她给孩子们写了一本书，但没多少孩子来看。

00:51:34.650 --> 00:51:45.750
<v Kirsten Gillibrand>Be strong and courageous. Do you know what the word courageous means? Do you know what it means? Courageous means brave. Do you know what the word--
<v Kirsten Gillibrand>要坚强和勇敢。你知道勇敢这个词是什么意思吗？你知道它是什么意思吗？勇敢意味着勇敢。你知道——这个词——

00:51:45.750 --> 00:51:49.840
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wow. What a coincidence. Brave just happens to be the theme of her entire campaign.
<v Ira Glass>哦，哇。多巧啊。勇敢恰好是她整个竞选的主题。

00:51:49.840 --> 00:51:58.440
<v Zoe Chace>That's exactly right. We went to an Iowa Democratic Veterans event where Eric Swalwell, a candidate for president, was supposed to be.
<v Zoe Chace>完全正确。我们去了爱荷华州民主党退伍军人活动，总统候选人埃里克·斯沃韦尔本该在那里。

00:51:58.440 --> 00:52:02.330
<v Zoe Chace>But can you just tell me what's going on? Is he coming? Where is he?
<v Zoe Chace>但你能告诉我发生了什么吗？他要来吗？他在哪？

00:52:02.330 --> 00:52:09.230
<v Joe Stutler>Eric Swalwell? Yeah, his flight was delayed. So staffer made it. He wanted to be here, but delayed flight.
<v Joe Stutler>埃里克·斯沃韦尔？是的，他的航班延误了。所以员工到了。他想在这里，但航班延误了。

00:52:09.230 --> 00:52:09.940
<v Zoe Chace>Got it.
<v Zoe Chace>明白了。

00:52:09.940 --> 00:52:16.200
<v Joe Stutler>We had that from several of them that had scheduling issues. Like, oh, crap, our flight's been bumped.
<v Joe Stutler>我们有几个有安排问题的人。比如，噢，糟了，我们的航班被挤掉了。

00:52:16.200 --> 00:52:17.300
<v Zoe Chace>Thank you.
<v Zoe Chace>谢谢。

00:52:17.300 --> 00:52:25.640
<v Joe Stutler>That's the silliness of waiting until the day of to get into a major event, as opposed to flying in a day early and being prepared.
<v Joe Stutler>这就是等到当天进入重大活动，而不是提前一天飞来做好准备的愚蠢之处。

00:52:25.640 --> 00:52:27.320
<v Ira Glass>Oh, he's mad.
<v Ira Glass>哦，他生气了。

00:52:27.320 --> 00:52:34.390
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, Joe Stutler, secretary of the Iowa Democratic Veterans Caucus. That's a guy you want on your side early on.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，乔·斯塔特勒，爱荷华州民主党退伍军人党团秘书。那是个你想要早点拉拢的人。

00:52:34.390 --> 00:52:40.350
<v Joe Stutler>I used to live on the road. It's like, you get there the day before, if it's important.
<v Joe Stutler>我过去经常在路上。它就像，如果你提前一天到达，如果它很重要。

00:52:40.350 --> 00:52:43.280
<v Zoe Chace>So you're kind of feeling like maybe this wasn't that important to him?
<v Zoe Chace>所以你觉得也许这对他来说没那么重要？

00:52:43.280 --> 00:52:57.230
<v Joe Stutler>No, no. I just think that they're all trying to do way too much. These are the lessons that we learn, sometimes the hard way.
<v Joe Stutler>不，不。我只是觉得他们都在试图做太多事情。这些是我们学到的教训，有时是痛苦的。

00:52:57.230 --> 00:53:06.860
<v Zoe Chace>Of course, there are lots of reporters who have covered these kinds of events a million times, who stand around afterwards nerding out about campaign history, which is kind of awesome.
<v Zoe Chace>当然，有很多报道过这类活动一百万次的记者，他们在事后聚在一起，像书呆子一样讨论竞选历史，这简直太棒了。

00:53:06.860 --> 00:53:10.480
<v Man 1>After Gene McCarthy finished a decent second event in New Hampshire--
<v Man 1>在尤金·麦卡锡在新罕布什尔州完成了一场体面的第二场活动后——

00:53:10.480 --> 00:53:13.170
<v Man 2>Close second to him in New Hampshire, and about to win--
<v Man 2>在新罕布什尔州紧随其后，而且即将获胜——

00:53:13.170 --> 00:53:14.270
<v Man 1>And then Bobby got it.
<v Man 1>然后鲍比得到了它。

00:53:14.270 --> 00:53:22.190
<v Man 2>And Bobby got it in, and then McCarthy was about to win Wisconsin, because Bobby had not gotten on the ballot, if I remember.
<v Man 2>然后鲍比得到了它，然后麦卡锡即将赢得威斯康星州，因为鲍比没能进入选票，如果我记得的话。

00:53:22.190 --> 00:53:23.300
<v Man 1>Right. Yeah.
<v Man 1>对。是的。

00:53:23.300 --> 00:53:41.240
<v Zoe Chace>And also, it's cool to see these guys talking about this moment-- this early moment in the campaign-- in this way, like it might be history later. It might not be, but they're talking about it like it is, like when Weigel and this other reporter were in the back of the room talking about Elizabeth Warren.
<v Zoe Chace>而且，看到这些家伙谈论这一刻——竞选的早期时刻——以这种方式，就像它以后可能是历史一样，也很酷。它可能不是，但他们谈论它就像它是，就像维格尔和另一位记者在房间后面谈论伊丽莎白·沃伦时一样。

00:53:41.240 --> 00:53:44.410
<v Reporter>I remember seeing her at the Sharpton thing.
<v Reporter>我记得在沙普顿的事情上见到她。

00:53:44.410 --> 00:53:44.790
<v Dave Weigel>Yeah.
<v Dave Weigel>是的。

00:53:44.790 --> 00:53:45.260
<v Reporter>The year before.
<v Reporter>前一年。

00:53:45.260 --> 00:53:46.630
<v Dave Weigel>Oh, I was there.
<v Dave Weigel>哦，我在那儿。

00:53:46.630 --> 00:53:49.220
<v Reporter>Yeah. And remember she had a really [INAUDIBLE] speaking time.
<v Reporter>是的。记得她有一次真正[听不清]的发言时间。

00:53:49.220 --> 00:53:51.920
<v Dave Weigel>I took out the dress. You all know the dress.
<v Dave Weigel>我拿出了那件衣服。你们都知道那件衣服。

00:53:51.920 --> 00:53:53.570
<v Reporter>And everybody went [BLEEP] nuts.
<v Reporter>然后每个人都[哔]疯了。

00:53:53.570 --> 00:53:53.860
<v Dave Weigel>Yeah.
<v Dave Weigel>是的。

00:53:53.860 --> 00:53:55.370
<v Zoe Chace>What was her dress line?
<v Zoe Chace>她的衣服台词是什么？

00:53:55.370 --> 00:54:11.420
<v Dave Weigel>So the dress line-- she tells it all the time-- is, when she was a kid-- and she always says, my mama and my daddy, because she's from Oklahoma. But she talks about, I heard my parents in that argument, talking at night after they thought I was asleep. And that's where I learned words like foreclosure and mortgage.
<v Dave Weigel>所以衣服台词——她总是讲这个——是，当她还是个孩子时——她总是说，我的妈妈和爸爸，因为她来自俄克拉荷马州。但她谈论，我听到我的父母在争论，在他们以为我睡着后的晚上谈话。那就是我学会“止赎”和“抵押”这些词的地方。

00:54:11.420 --> 00:54:12.030
<v Zoe Chace>Right.
<v Zoe Chace>对。

00:54:12.030 --> 00:54:30.870
<v Dave Weigel>And I walked past my mother's room, and I saw her puttering-- going back and forth with a dress laid out. And she goes, y'all know the dress. It's the one you only use for graduations and funerals-- weddings, graduations, and funerals. And she looks at the dress and says, we're not going to lose this house. We're not going to lose this house.
<v Dave Weigel>我走过我母亲的房间，我看到她来回踱步，摆着一件裙子。她说，你们都知道那件裙子。它是你只用于毕业典礼和葬礼——婚礼、毕业典礼和葬礼。她看着裙子说，我们不会失去这所房子。我们不会失去这所房子。

00:54:30.870 --> 00:54:33.210
<v Reporter>She does it like in this-- like she's doing it kind of like--
<v Reporter>她这样做就像在——她这样做就像——

00:54:33.210 --> 00:54:34.340
<v Dave Weigel>Like a Tennessee Williams character.
<v Dave Weigel>像田纳西·威廉姆斯笔下的角色。

00:54:34.340 --> 00:54:34.850
<v Reporter>She's, like--
<v Reporter>她——

00:54:34.850 --> 00:54:35.890
<v Dave Weigel>Yeah.
<v Dave Weigel>是的。

00:54:35.890 --> 00:54:44.840
<v Zoe Chace>And the very next day, I saw her give this speech to a pretty big crowd in a backyard in Waterloo, Iowa. Everybody was just kind of rapt.
<v Zoe Chace>第二天，我看到她在爱荷华州滑铁卢的一个后院给一大群人演讲。每个人都相当入迷。

00:54:44.840 --> 00:55:05.240
<v Elizabeth Warren>And there was the dress, and there was my mother. She was in her slip and her stocking feet. And she was pacing and crying, just talking to herself and saying, we will not lose this house. We will not lose this house. We will not lose this house.
<v Elizabeth Warren>那件裙子在那里，我的母亲也在那里。她穿着内衣，袜子脚。她在踱步和哭泣，只是自言自语说，我们不会失去这所房子。我们不会失去这所房子。我们不会失去这所房子。

00:55:05.240 --> 00:55:25.100
<v Zoe Chace>And this one guy was wiping tears away. At this point, Weigel can sort of divide the candidates into two groups. And it's not the moderates and the far left. He says, actually, the voters he talks to are less hung up on the policy questions. What they mainly want is to be able to close their eyes and see this person beating Donald Trump.
<v Zoe Chace>那个家伙在擦眼泪。在这一点上，维格尔可以将候选人分成两组。这不是温和派和极左派。他说，事实上，他谈话的选民对政策问题不那么纠结。他们主要想要的是能够闭上眼睛，看到这个人击败唐纳德·特朗普。

00:55:25.100 --> 00:55:25.950
<v Ira Glass>Hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:55:25.950 --> 00:55:40.010
<v Zoe Chace>The two groups he notices are, one, the candidates where people show up at one of their events, and a transformation happens. The voter leaves the event excited, inspired. The other group are the ones where that transformation doesn't happen.
<v Zoe Chace>他注意到的两组是，一，选民出现在他们的活动上，发生转变的候选人。选民离开活动时感到兴奋、受到启发。另一组是没发生那种转变的人。

00:55:40.010 --> 00:55:42.050
<v Ira Glass>So who's in the excited group?
<v Ira Glass>所以谁在兴奋组里？

00:55:42.050 --> 00:55:45.720
<v Zoe Chace>Cory Booker, Pete Buttigieg, and most of all, Elizabeth Warren.
<v Zoe Chace>科里·布克、皮特·布蒂吉格，最重要的是，伊丽莎白·沃伦。

00:55:45.720 --> 00:55:46.400
<v Ira Glass>Huh.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:55:46.400 --> 00:55:49.330
<v Zoe Chace>People see her, and they're surprised at how much they like her.
<v Zoe Chace>人们看到她，对他们竟然如此喜欢她感到惊讶。

00:55:49.330 --> 00:55:51.350
<v Ira Glass>Huh. And the unexcited group?
<v Ira Glass>嗯。那么不兴奋的组呢？

00:55:51.350 --> 00:56:00.790
<v Zoe Chace>Basically everyone else, including the front runner, Joe Biden. People would come to see him because they already loved him, and then they would leave feeling no different, or worse.
<v Zoe Chace>基本上其他人，包括领跑者乔·拜登。人们去看他，因为他们已经爱他了，然后他们离开时感觉没什么不同，或者更糟。

00:56:00.790 --> 00:56:02.930
<v Ira Glass>Like they want to be inspired, but they weren't inspired.
<v Ira Glass>就像他们想受到启发，但他们没受到启发。

00:56:02.930 --> 00:56:06.350
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah. Beto-- people went backwards.
<v Zoe Chace>是的。贝托——人们倒退了。

00:56:06.350 --> 00:56:14.070
<v Dave Weigel>I have all these memories, which already seem surreal, of Beto O'Rourke coming to somewhere and there being an enormous crowd to see him.
<v Dave Weigel>我所有这些记忆，看起来已经很超现实，关于贝托·奥罗克来到某处，那里有巨大的人群看着他。

00:56:14.070 --> 00:56:19.460
<v Zoe Chace>Now it's like the crowd is smaller, and people leave underwhelmed.
<v Zoe Chace>现在人群变小了，人们离开时感到失望。

00:56:19.460 --> 00:56:21.170
<v Ira Glass>And what does he see changing?
<v Ira Glass>他看到什么在改变？

00:56:21.170 --> 00:56:25.430
<v Zoe Chace>Since the first debate, at this point, the field is basically sorting itself out.
<v Zoe Chace>自第一次辩论以来，在这一点上，战场基本上正在自我整理。

00:56:25.430 --> 00:56:43.910
<v Dave Weigel>The debates clarify that some people don't have secret candidate charisma powers that are going to reveal themselves at some point. They haven't yet. It might not be happening. So I think that the field probably is going to limit itself more to 16, 17 people soon.
<v Dave Weigel>辩论澄清了有些人没有秘密候选人魅力力量，这会在某点揭示出来。他们还没有。它可能不会发生。所以我认为战场可能很快会把自己限制在16、17个人。

00:56:43.910 --> 00:57:01.340
<v Dave Weigel>When it comes to me taking people seriously, if at this point you had two fundraising quarters, you're in the teens or the high single digits, and you have enough money to fund 50 staff in Iowa, I think that's more interesting. And that's more important than, hey, there's another guy who's at 0%, and he's meeting at a diner.
<v Dave Weigel>说到我认真对待人们，如果此时你有两次筹款季度，你在十几岁或高个位数，并且你有足够的钱资助爱荷华州的50名员工，我认为那更有趣。那比，“嘿，有另一个家伙在0%，他在餐馆开会”更重要。

00:57:01.340 --> 00:57:07.370
<v Ira Glass>Aw. So the special magical time where it seems like any of the two dozen candidates could rise, that's ending?
<v Ira Glass>噢。所以似乎任何两打候选人都能崛起的特殊神奇时间，要结束了？

00:57:07.370 --> 00:57:18.200
<v Zoe Chace>It is, except there's a dark horse out there still who could shake things up. There is one more move left in this great game of who will run America.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，除非外面还有一匹黑马可能会改变局面。在这个谁将统治美国的伟大游戏中，还剩下最后一步棋。

00:57:18.200 --> 00:57:33.050
<v Ira Glass>I know where you're going with this. Yes, on the Republican side, things are still wide open and away. There, it is just the president and so far, one other candidate, former governor Bill Weld. Weld held elective office 20 years ago. He so far has not had much of an impact.
<v Ira Glass>我知道你要去哪儿。是的，在共和党这边，事情仍然很广泛。在那里，只有总统和到目前为止，另一位候选人，前州长比尔·韦尔德。韦尔德在20年前担任过选举职位。到目前为止他还没产生太大影响。

00:57:33.050 --> 00:57:36.890
<v Ira Glass>And for our last story, we are joined now by one of our producers, David Kestenbaum. Hey, David.
<v Ira Glass>对于我们的最后一个故事，我们的制片人之一戴维·凯斯滕鲍姆现在加入了我们。嘿，戴维。

00:57:36.890 --> 00:57:37.760
<v David Kestenbaum>Hey, Ira.
<v David Kestenbaum>嘿，艾拉。

00:57:37.760 --> 00:57:49.090
<v Ira Glass>So you talked to two Republicans who are doing basically everything they can to find a genuine heavy hitter to run against the President of the United States from within his own party.
<v Ira Glass>所以你谈到了两位共和党人，他们基本上正在尽一切可能找到一个真正的重量级人物在自己的政党内与美国总统竞争。

00:57:49.090 --> 00:57:53.970
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, they feel like if the right person were just to enter the race, it could matter more than anything the Democrats are doing.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的，他们觉得如果合适的人选进入竞选，这可能比民主党所做的任何事情都重要。

00:57:53.970 --> 00:57:54.740
<v Ira Glass>Hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:57:54.740 --> 00:57:57.590
<v David Kestenbaum>So here's the scene, an office in Washington, DC.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以这是场景，华盛顿特区的一个办公室。

00:57:57.590 --> 00:58:01.590
<v Sarah Longwell>Hey, Bill? Hey, Carson. Is Bill out there?
<v Sarah Longwell>嘿，比尔？嘿，卡森。比尔在外面吗？

00:58:01.590 --> 00:58:21.890
<v David Kestenbaum>That's Sarah Longwell. She's a Republican strategist. The mug in front of her on the desk reads, "These are the tears of my staff." And it's filled with pens. The Bill she is looking for is Bill Kristol, the guy from TV, the one that seems to have an encyclopedic memory of every election in American history. Kristol worked in the first Bush administration, helped start the Weekly Standard magazine.
<v David Kestenbaum>那是莎拉·朗威尔。她是共和党战略家。她桌上杯子写着，“这是我员工的眼泪。”里面装满了笔。她寻找的比尔是比尔·克里斯托尔，电视上的那个家伙，那个似乎对美国历史上每场选举都有百科全书式记忆的人。克里斯托尔在布什政府工作，帮助创办了《每周标准》杂志。

00:58:21.890 --> 00:58:24.440
<v Sarah Longwell>Oh, you had to stop and get your fancy bougie coffee.
<v Sarah Longwell>哦，你不得不停下来买你那杯花哨的波西米亚咖啡。

00:58:24.440 --> 00:58:30.260
<v David Kestenbaum>He wanders in and sits down on a small couch. They've just done a poll of voters in New Hampshire, and they're meeting to talk about it.
<v David Kestenbaum>他溜进办公室，坐在小沙发上。他们刚刚在新罕布什尔州做了一次选民民调，他们正开会谈论这件事。

00:58:30.260 --> 00:58:36.080
<v Sarah Longwell>I mean, look. If I was looking at this thinking about running for the Republican nomination--
<v Sarah Longwell>我的意思是，看。如果我在考虑竞选共和党提名——

00:58:36.080 --> 00:58:38.190
<v Bill Kristol>You should do that. I say this all the time.
<v Bill Kristol>你应该那样做。我一直这么说。

00:58:38.190 --> 00:58:38.780
<v Sarah Longwell>Yeah, right.
<v Sarah Longwell>是的，对吧。

00:58:38.780 --> 00:58:44.600
<v Bill Kristol>We have all these other people we're talking to-- congressmen, and ex-senators, and all these big shots.
<v Bill Kristol>我们有所有其他我们在交谈的人——众议员、前参议员，以及所有这些大人物。

00:58:44.600 --> 00:58:48.830
<v Sarah Longwell>This is his great pitch, that I should run. We are really in dire straits, if that's where we're at now.
<v Sarah Longwell>这是他的伟大推销，我应该参选。如果我们现在处于那种地步，那我们真的处于严峻的困境。

00:58:48.830 --> 00:58:50.070
<v Bill Kristol>Totally wrong.
<v Bill Kristol>完全错了。

00:58:50.070 --> 00:59:13.010
<v David Kestenbaum>I think if you were taking on a standing president from within your own party, there are kind of two paths. You can do it quietly, out of view, or you can do it the way Kristol has-- by never shutting up about it on television and newspaper articles. He recently tweeted that he'd been accused of being in a secret cabal to find a challenger to Trump. His answer-- there's nothing secret about it.
<v David Kestenbaum>我认为如果你在自己的政党内挑战一位现任总统，有两种途径。你可以静悄悄地，视线之外做，或者你可以像克里斯托尔那样——通过在电视和报纸文章中永远不闭嘴来做。他最近推特说他被指控是一个秘密阴谋集团的一员来寻找特朗普的挑战者。他的回答——关于它没什么秘密的。

00:59:13.010 --> 00:59:40.160
<v David Kestenbaum>Kristol and Longwell have raised a few million dollars in the past two years. And they've been using it to try and see, is this thing even possible? The whole idea of running against Trump in the primary can seem a little crazy. He has like an 80% or 90% approval rating from Republicans. But they feel like it is not crazy at all. In that poll they just had done of Republicans in New Hampshire and independents who might vote in the Republican primary, half were open to voting for a challenger. Half.
<v David Kestenbaum>克里斯托尔和朗威尔在过去两年筹集了数百万美元。他们一直在用它试图看看，这件事甚至可能吗？竞选共和党初选挑战特朗普的整个想法看起来有点疯狂。他在共和党人中有80%或90%的支持率。但他们觉得这完全不疯狂。在他们对新罕布什尔州共和党人和可能在共和党初选中投票的独立人士进行的民调中，一半人对投票给挑战者持开放态度。一半。

00:59:40.160 --> 01:00:02.570
<v Sarah Longwell>No, I mean, I was just up in Manchester, and I did a series of focus groups. If you ask them specifically, hey, would you be open to an alternative in 2020? Would you like to see another Republican candidate? Every single time, nine out of the 10 hands go up. I think for some people, there's a hunger for an alternative. And then for other people, it's just an openness, like a willingness for political competition.
<v Sarah Longwell>不，我的意思是，我刚才在曼彻斯特，我做了一系列焦点小组。如果你明确问他们，嘿，你会对2020年的替代方案持开放态度吗？你想看到另一位共和党候选人吗？每一次，10个人中有9个举手。我认为对一些人来说，有一种对替代方案的渴望。然后对其他人来说，这只是开放性，就像一种对政治竞争的意愿。

01:00:02.570 --> 01:00:14.040
<v David Kestenbaum>One thing that really surprises people in these focus groups, she says, is when you explain just how much the debt has gone up under Trump. It's increased more during the first two years of Trump than it did the previous two years under Obama.
<v David Kestenbaum>她说，当人们解释特朗普领导下的债务增加了多少时，这真的让焦点小组中的人们感到惊讶。特朗普前两年增加的债务比奥巴马前两年增加的还多。

01:00:14.040 --> 01:00:28.710
<v David Kestenbaum>Bill Kristol lays out how this all might go for a challenger. You raise a little money, go to Iowa, New Hampshire. Maybe you get 30% of the vote in New Hampshire. That is a serious blow to the president. You're off and running. So that's their case.
<v David Kestenbaum>比尔·克里斯托尔列出了挑战者会发生的一切。你筹集一点钱，去爱荷华州、新罕布什尔州。也许你获得30%的新罕布什尔州选票。这对总统来说是一个沉重的打击。你出发了。所以这就是他们的案例。

01:00:28.710 --> 01:00:42.320
<v David Kestenbaum>There is one kind of noticeable challenge to this plan. Since we've had the current primary system, there are zero examples-- zero-- of someone challenging a sitting president in a primary and actually winning.
<v David Kestenbaum>这个计划有一个相当明显的挑战。自从我们有了目前的初选制度以来，有零个例子——零——有人在初选中挑战现任总统并真正获胜。

01:00:42.320 --> 01:00:48.690
<v David Kestenbaum>I mean, is some part of this just kind of like theater? And I mean theater you truly believe in?
<v David Kestenbaum>我的意思是，其中一部分只是像戏剧吗？我的意思是，你真正相信的戏剧？

01:00:48.690 --> 01:01:09.750
<v Bill Kristol>I mean, I don't want to say yes because of course-- because that really isn't. I don't think of it that way. I'm not a very theatrical person. But I guess theater in the sense that presidential politics is the biggest stage we have in politics. And the presidential contest is the biggest stage. And I don't want to simply leave that stage on the Republican side alone to Donald Trump.
<v Bill Kristol>我的意思是，我不想说是，因为当然——因为那确实不是。我不那样想它。我不是一个非常有戏剧性的人。但我猜戏剧的意义在于，总统政治是我们政治中最大的舞台。而总统竞选是最大的舞台。我不想仅仅把共和党那边的舞台留给唐纳德·特朗普。

01:01:09.750 --> 01:01:22.360
<v David Kestenbaum>I'm still trying to think through just, like in your mind, why you're doing this. And is part of it just like a feeling like what is democracy for, if I can't cast a vote for the person I really want to be in office?
<v David Kestenbaum>我还在试图思考——在你的脑子里，你为什么做这个。一部分是因为感觉民主是为了什么，如果我不能把我真正想要的人投进办公室？

01:01:22.360 --> 01:01:38.040
<v Bill Kristol>Yeah, I think it's actually people are overthinking this. I mean, I'm doing it because I don't think Trump should have a second term. I'm a Republican. It'd be a heck of a thing to say, OK, you think he's really leading the country down a terrible path, and you should just take two or four or six years off.
<v Bill Kristol>是的，我想实际上人们过度思考了这个。我的意思是，我做这个是因为我不认为特朗普应该有第二个任期。我是共和党人。如果说，哦，你认为他真的在引导国家走上一条可怕的道路，你应该仅仅休假两、四或六年，那真是太糟糕了。

01:01:38.040 --> 01:01:40.740
<v David Kestenbaum>But you're really unlikely to succeed, right?
<v David Kestenbaum>但你成功的可能性很小，对吧？

01:01:40.740 --> 01:01:43.090
<v Bill Kristol>Pretty unlikely to succeed, yeah.
<v Bill Kristol>成功的可能性相当小，是的。

01:01:43.090 --> 01:01:44.550
<v David Kestenbaum>So why do it?
<v David Kestenbaum>所以为什么要这样做？

01:01:44.550 --> 01:01:48.290
<v Bill Kristol>Look. The last three presidents were not primaried, and they won re-election.
<v Bill Kristol>看。过去三位总统没有参加初选，他们赢得了连任。

01:01:48.290 --> 01:02:23.870
<v David Kestenbaum>And here, Bill offered up what seems to be the other math that candidates are doing. The last three presidents did not have a primary challenge, and they won re-election. When a sitting president has been challenged by his own party, like Carter by Kennedy, Ford by Reagan, Bush by Buchanan, the challenger doesn't win. But maybe because they damaged the president, he does not win re-election. In other words, this may be the bargain. You can run for president, possibly shape history, end Trump, change who wins, but you don't get to win.
<v David Kestenbaum>在这里，比尔提出了候选人们正在做的似乎是另一种数学。过去三位总统没有受到初选挑战，他们赢得了连任。当现任总统受到自己政党的挑战时，比如卡特受到肯尼迪、福特受到里根、布什受到布坎南，挑战者并没有赢。但也许因为他们破坏了总统，他没能赢得连任。换句话说，这可能是这种交易。你可以竞选总统，可能塑造历史，结束特朗普，改变获胜者，但你没有赢得胜利。

01:02:23.870 --> 01:02:39.100
<v David Kestenbaum>I mean, it's interesting. The other Republicans I talked to pointed out that history is not on your side. And you were pointing out, oh, it is on my side, in the sense that a sitting president challenged tends to lose. But that means what you're doing is just an act of internal sabotage.
<v David Kestenbaum>我的意思是，这很有趣。我谈到的其他共和党人指出，历史并不站在你这一边。而你指出，哦，它站在我这一边，意义在于一位现任总统受到挑战往往会输。但这意味着你正在做的只是内部破坏行为。

01:02:39.100 --> 01:02:43.160
<v Bill Kristol>No, but I think it's also holding out an alternative in a good way. But yeah--
<v Bill Kristol>不，但我认为这同时也是以一种好的方式保持替代方案。但是的——

01:02:43.160 --> 01:02:44.350
<v David Kestenbaum>Or sabotage. I mean--
<v David Kestenbaum>或者破坏。我的意思是——

01:02:44.350 --> 01:02:50.130
<v Bill Kristol>Well, people can say that. They said that about the French who didn't go along with Vichy. I mean, what are you supposed to do? If you think it's bad--
<v Bill Kristol>嗯，人们可以那样说。他们那样说那些没跟维希政权走的法国人。我的意思是，你应该怎么做？如果你认为这很糟糕——

01:02:50.130 --> 01:02:52.030
<v David Kestenbaum>I love that every time I have a point that's historical--
<v David Kestenbaum>我喜欢每次我有历史观点的时刻——

01:02:52.030 --> 01:03:14.380
<v Bill Kristol>If you think it's bad for the country, they can say it's sabotage. I would say it's opposition. I don't think it's sabotage. Sabotage implies secrecy and deception. I don't think there's a heck of a lot of secrecy and deception in what I'm doing. I think I've been pretty open about my opposition, so I feel like I'm-- isn't that the point of America?
<v Bill Kristol>如果你认为这对国家很糟糕，他们可以说这是破坏。我会说这是反对。我不认为这是破坏。破坏意味着秘密和欺骗。我不认为我在做的事情里有太多秘密和欺骗。我想我已经对比我的反对非常公开，所以我感觉我是——那不是美国的意义吗？

01:03:14.380 --> 01:03:49.430
<v Bill Kristol>Honestly, if I were a Democrat, I would call me up and say, here's a lot of money for a primary challenge-- and we have talked to a lot of Democrats about this, obviously-- against Donald Trump. I mean, that's actually the single best thing you can do to weaken Trump. You can help Elizabeth Warren, or Kamala Harris, or Cory Booker right now. But we don't know which of those will actually be a good nominee against Trump. The one thing you do know is if Trump has to spend money against the primary challenger, if he loses 32% of the vote in New Hampshire, which is entirely possible, and it's embarrassing, it would be helpful to weaken Trump.
<v Bill Kristol>老实说，如果我是民主党人，我会给我打电话说，给你很多钱进行初选挑战——当然我们已经和很多民主党人谈过这个——挑战唐纳德·特朗普。我的意思是，这实际上是你可以削弱特朗普所能做的唯一最好的事情。你可以现在帮助伊丽莎白·沃伦、卡玛拉·哈里斯或科里·布克。但我们不知道他们中哪一个会真正成为针对特朗普的好候选人。你知道的一件事是，如果特朗普必须花钱对抗初选挑战者，如果他失去32%的新罕布什尔州选票，那是完全可能的，而且这很尴尬，削弱特朗普将是有帮助的。

01:03:49.430 --> 01:03:56.890
<v David Kestenbaum>Wait, but is your goal to run somebody who's going to win the nomination and maybe the presidency? Or is your goal just to take down Trump?
<v David Kestenbaum>等等，但你的目标是推举一个会赢得提名并可能赢得总统竞选的人吗？还是你的目标仅仅是打倒特朗普？

01:03:56.890 --> 01:04:06.430
<v Bill Kristol>Both. Obviously, the first would be the best. But I would regard the second as an adequate result, and I think actually an important result, if I can say.
<v Bill Kristol>两者都是。很明显，第一个会是最好的。但我会把第二个看作是一个足够的结果，而且如果我可以这么说的话，我认为那实际上是一个重要的结果。

01:04:06.430 --> 01:04:13.780
<v David Kestenbaum>Sarah Longwell has been weighing all this, too. It's possible the whole thing could backfire if the wrong Democrat ended up in the White House.
<v David Kestenbaum>莎拉·朗威尔也一直在权衡这一切。如果错误的民主党人最终进入白宫，整件事可能会适得其反。

01:04:13.780 --> 01:04:19.490
<v Sarah Longwell>I certainly would not want Bernie Sanders to be the President of the United States.
<v Sarah Longwell>我当然不希望伯尼·桑德斯成为美国总统。

01:04:19.490 --> 01:04:23.240
<v David Kestenbaum>So if you ended up handing it to Sanders, that would not be a happy day for you.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以如果你最终把它交给了桑德斯，那对你来说不会是快乐的一天。

01:04:23.240 --> 01:04:40.160
<v Sarah Longwell>Well, that's sort of like, do I want to be poisoned, or do I want to be shot? Those are just sort of bad choices. And I think America deserves better than to have choices between an old socialist and sort of an authoritarian sort of nativist. My choice is neither.
<v Sarah Longwell>嗯，那就像，我是想被毒死，还是想被枪杀？那些只是一些糟糕的选择。而且我认为美国值得比在老社会主义者和某种威权主义的本土主义者之间做出选择更好的选择。我的选择是两者皆非。

01:04:40.160 --> 01:04:58.730
<v David Kestenbaum>There's this scene in the HBO series on Chernobyl that's running right now where the nuclear reactor is about to explode. So these workers have to go into the basement of the power plant to try to open a valve. There's so much radiation, it seems certain they're going to die, but it will also save everyone else.
<v David Kestenbaum>目前正在播出的HBO切尔诺贝利系列剧中有这样一个场景，核反应堆即将爆炸。所以这些工人必须进入核电厂的地下室试图打开一个阀门。辐射太严重了，他们似乎肯定会死，但这也会拯救其他人。

01:04:58.730 --> 01:05:25.790
<v David Kestenbaum>Sarah has been watching the show. I asked her if she ever thought about it like that. No, she said. Never. They're not asking someone to risk their life, they're asking someone to put their name in the hat to be the next president of the United States. Apparently, it's easier to get someone to walk into Chernobyl.
<v David Kestenbaum>莎拉一直在看这部剧。我问她是否曾想过像那样。不，她说。从来没有。他们没有要求某人冒生命危险，他们要求某人把名字放进帽子里成为下一任美国总统。显然，找人走进切尔诺贝利更容易。

01:05:25.790 --> 01:05:45.610
<v David Kestenbaum>[MUSIC - STEVE FORBERT, "YOU CANNOT WIN IF YOU DO NOT PLAY"]
<v David Kestenbaum>[音乐 - 史蒂夫·福伯特，“如果你不玩就赢不了”]

